\subsection{Layer $>0$ CompNode Hidden Security}
\label{subsubsec:security_layer_>_0}

We now turn our attention to CompNode security of $\boldsymbol{h}^{R_i}$ at deeper layers of the LLM. Can we select a sharding strategy that defends against our attack outlined in \cref{sec:walkthrough}? Also, do we remain secure to learning-based attacks as in prior works \citet{wan2024informationleakageembeddinglarge} and \citet{ morris2023languagemodelinversion}?






\textbf{Vocab-Matching Attack}\hspace{1em} Our defense against vocab-matching is to ensure large token gaps in nodes. For a $(c,\delta)$-sharding scheme, in each shard, the distance from one `cluster' of indices to the next is $\delta - c + 1$. Therefore, when one carries out the generalized vocab-matching attack, the cost scales exponentially with the maximum gap, so it is on the order of $V^{\delta - c + 1}$. Note $\delta-c+1=(\alpha-1)c+1$, so regardless of the value of the vocab-matching threshold $\rho$, we can increase $\alpha$ or $c$ until $\delta-c+1\geq \rho$ to prevent vocab-matching. For $\rho = 3$, all $\alpha,c \geq 2$ satisfy this.

One objection to this line of argument is that if the adversary has a strong prior on the input text distribution, they can eliminate unlikely tokens from forward passes in the generalized attack, essentially only iterating over tokens in $\mathcal{V}_0 \subseteq \mathcal{V}$. However, the cost of this optimized attack is on the order of  $|\mathcal{V}_0|^{\delta-c+1}$, so it still scales exponentially. Thus, even if $|\mathcal{V}_0|$ is hundreds of times smaller than $|\mathcal{V}|\sim 100000$, large enough $\rho$ ensures that $\delta-c+1 \geq \rho$ remains secure.

% \newmethod{} sharding is motivated by defense against the vocab-matching attack of 
% \cref{sec:hidden_reversal}. To make this precise, we consider a theoretical strengthening of vocab-matching called \textit{$\delta$-fold vocab-matching}. In vanilla vocab-matching, the adversary uses knowledge of tokens $<i$ to deduce token $i$ through a linear search of vocabulary $\mathcal{V}$, but any missing token $<i$ would prevent their attack. The goal of $\delta$-fold vocab-matching is to reverse token $i$ even if some tokens before are missing, by looking through all phrases of $\delta$ consecutive vocabulary elements rather than just individual vocabulary elements. That is, the linear search is done over $\mathcal{V}^\delta$. 

% The search space is already quite large for small $\delta$: for Gemma-2-2B-IT, since $|\mathcal{V}|=256,000$, we have $|\mathcal{V}^\delta|\geq 10^{20}$ for $\delta \geq 4$. One potential objection to this measure of security is that the search space can be reduced by using only reasonable phrases and context from surrounding tokens. However, our results in \cref{sec:permuted_decoding} and \cref{sec:noised_decoding} suggest LLM hidden states are highly non-colliding even under obfuscations like permutation and noising: so they provide strong evidence that a since vocab embeddings usually don't have a nice explicit structure.

% We define the \textit{token delta} $\delta$ as the maximum positive integer where an adversary could carry out the $\delta$-fold vocab-matching attack on \textit{any} sentence. 

\textbf{Learning-Based Attacks}\hspace{1em} We now consider learning-based reversal attacks. Although hidden states may not be as easily reversible as token embeddings, it may be the case that as attention propagates information among hidden states, the text-infilling task is easier at deeper layers of the LLM than at the purely textual level. We examine this hypothesis by fine-tuning Gemma-2-2B-IT and Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct on $(c,\delta)$-masked input sequences from FineWeb-Edu, with the target labels being the full input sequence. Note this is a `worst-case' scenario where the adversary knows the $c$ and $\delta$ parameters. We update both models to use a bidirectional mask, in line with the token-infilling nature of this task. We train until the evaluation loss on a held-out set converges over layer 1 representations from each model, and evaluate on layer 1 hidden states. Our approach is similar to  \citet{wan2024informationleakageembeddinglarge, morris2023languagemodelinversion}.

