\textit{Harmonically driven interradical motion.}---
Following the approach in \cite{drive22}, we introduce radical motion
\begin{align}
r(t) = \frac{\Delta_{\mathrm{d}}}{2}[1-\cos(2\pi \nu_{\mathrm{d}}t)] + r_0,
\end{align}
where $\nu_{\mathrm{d}}$ is the driving frequency and the driving amplitude is set to $\Delta_{\mathrm{d}} = 3\,$\AA, as it was previously found to produce significant sensitivity enhancements while constituting a relatively modest motion. $r_0=17.2\,$\AA\ specifies the interradical distance of the static FAD$^{\bullet -}$/W$_{\mathrm{C}}^{\bullet+}$ radical-pair \cite{Gruning2022_Dynamical}. This radical motion modulates the dipolar coupling as
\begin{align}
        \hat{H}(t)_{\rm Dipolar} =& - d(r(t))\Big[3(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_1 \cdot \mathbf{u})(\hat{\mathbf{S}}_2\cdot \mathbf{u}) - \hat{\mathbf{S}}_1 \cdot \hat{\mathbf{S}}_2\Big],
    \label{eq:eed_hamiltonian}
\end{align}
where $d(r(t)) \equiv \mu_0g_e^2\mu_B^2/4\pi r(t)^3 > 0$, with $r\equiv|\mathbf{r}|$ and constants taking their usual definitions, and $\mathbf{u}=\mathbf{r}/r$. The recombination \cite{drive22} is modulated as
\begin{align}
k_{\mathrm{b}}(t) = k_{\mathrm{b_{0}}} \exp[-\beta (r(t)-r_0)],
\end{align}
The exchange interaction is modulated in the same functional form as
\begin{align}
    \hat{H}(t)_{\mathrm{Exchange}} = -2 J(t) \hat{\mathbf{S}}_1 \cdot \hat{\mathbf{S}}_2, \label{eq:exchange_hamiltonian}
\end{align}
with $J(t) = J_{0} \exp[-\beta (r(t)-r_0)]$. Together with Eqs.~\ref{eq:H0} and \ref{eq:H1} these define  
\begin{align}
    \hat{H}_{\mathrm{eff}}(t) = \hat{H}(t) - i\left( \frac{k_{b}(t)}{2}\hat{P}_{\mathrm{S}} + \frac{k_{f}}{2}\hat{I}\right). \label{eq:eff_hamiltonian}
\end{align}
