\textit{Robustness to hyperfine complexity.}---
While it is known that the magnitude of relative anisotropy decreases \cite{Gruning2022_Dynamical} as the number of hyperfine coupled nuclei is increased in FAD$^{\bullet -}$/W$_{\mathrm{C}}^{\bullet+}$, we show that the factor of enhancement from driving persists \cite{drive22}. To see how robust the approach to the QCRB is to system complexity in the driven system, we increase the number of hyperfine couplings in the radical-pair to four (two in each radical), adding the next most relevant hyperfine couplings N$10$ and H$1$. Fig.~\ref{Fig:robustness} shows the ratio of $F_{\theta}/\mathcal{F}_{\theta}$, over a wide range of $200$ driving frequencies in the range $1\leq \nu_{\mathrm{d}} \leq 100\,$MHz in the presence of EED interactions with $J_{0}/2\pi = 0\,$MHz, where insets show select driving values of $\nu_{\mathrm{d}}=1.5,\ 8, \ \& \ 35\,$MHz where $F_{\theta}/\mathcal{F}_{\theta}$ peaks, extended over the range $-100\leq J_{0}/2\pi \leq 100\,$MHz. Here, only orientations of $\theta$ and $\phi$ that maximize and minimize the yield difference of the static system are scanned, to allow the dense and wide parameter analysis over exchange coupling and driving frequency. Despite this minimal scan, close approaches to saturating the QCRB are reached (up to $F_{\theta}/\mathcal{F}_{\theta}=0.930$). In the SM we demonstrate similar degrees of optimality of a reduced scan of the two-nucleus radical-pair, suggesting that the approach to the QCRB from interradical fluctuations is maintained as hyperfine couplings are added.

\textit{Optimal quantum control.}--- We discretize our control functions into piecewise-constant segments, as standard in gradient-based approaches \cite{dey25, oqc05}. The total Hamiltonian including the drift and control parts, with $u_j$ constant during each interval, is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:discrete_hamiltonian}
\hat{H}_{\mathrm{Total}}(u_j) = \hat{H}_{\mathrm{Drift}} + f_{\mathrm{A}}(u_j)\hat{H}_{\mathrm{A}} + f_\mathrm{B}(u_j)\hat{H}_{\mathrm{B}},
\end{equation}
where $f_\mathrm{A}$ considers the exchange interaction with an exponential dependence (as in Eq.~\ref{eq:exchange_hamiltonian}), and $f_\mathrm{B}$ considers the dipolar with an inverse cubic dependence (as in Eq.~\ref{eq:eed_hamiltonian}). Following the approach from \cite{cont24}, the controls are optimized to maximize the difference in yields: $\Phi_{\mathrm{S}, \mathrm{max}} - \Phi_{\mathrm{S}, \mathrm{min}}$, with min and max in this case referring to the magnetic field orientations of the minimal and maximal yield respectively in the absence of the controlled distance fluctuations. In practice, the resulting optimization problem $\max_{\mathbf{u}} J(\mathbf{u})$ is numerically solved by transforming into a minimization form, with $\mathbf{u}$ mapped to displacement of the radicals from their position in the crystal structure, while enforcing boundedness and smoothness. We use 1000 controls of $1\mskip3mu$ns each, whereby the maximal displacement is bound to $3\,\mathrm{\AA}$, ensuring physical feasibility of controls. 

\textit{Angular precision magnitudes.}---To translate our findings into explicit estimates of angular precision, we use the energy resolution limit \cite{Kominis2025_EnergyResolutionAnimal} to estimate the minimum number of magnetoreceptors required for sensing. For radical-pair lifetimes in the range $1$--$10\,\mu$s, this corresponds to an effective number of active magnetoreceptors between $N=2\times10^{6}$ and $N=2\times10^{5}$, in line with previous estimates \cite{Hiscock2019_Navigating_Night}. We then calculate the total classical Fisher information and use the Cram\'{e}r-Rao bound to estimate the angular precision $\Delta\theta=\sqrt{NF_{\theta}}$ obtainable from singlet and triplet measurements in the representative FAD$^{\bullet -}$/W$_{\mathrm{C}}^{\bullet+}$ system. Using this approach, we find angular precisions ranging from $0.101^{\circ}$--$0.319^{\circ}$ under harmonic driving, and $0.034^{\circ}$--$0.107^{\circ}$ with quantum-control-optimized interradical perturbations. For the static case, optimal precision ranges from $0.214^{\circ}$--$0.675^{\circ}$, however, for the representative scenario of $J_{0}/2\pi=0\,$MHz, the performance deteriorates to as low as $9.11^{\circ}$ -- $28.8^{\circ}$, corresponding to $NF_{\theta}= 1.98 \times10^{-5}$. 

By analyzing how precision degrades with increasing hyperfine complexity (e.g., $NF_{\theta}= 0.029$, corresponding to $0.238^{\circ}$--$0.752^{\circ}$ at four couplings), we extrapolate that in more biologically realistic regimes \cite{qTr25} with tens of hyperfine couplings, the CFI under harmonic driving ($NF_{\theta} \sim 10^{-3}$--$10^{-4}$) would yield angular precision on the order of a few degrees. Although RFR noise would lead to further degradation by roughly an order of magnitude in the CFI, structured perturbations, as in the quantum-controlled system, could more than compensate for this. In contrast, a static system with similar complexity performs far worse: previous analysis shows $NF_{\theta} \sim 1\times 10^{-7}$ even without RFR, such that formally the angular precision amounts to $128$ -- $405^{\circ}$. Finally, using the QFI, the fundamental bounds on angular precision are estimated as $0.007^{\circ}$ -- $0.0225^{\circ}$ for the idealized single nucleus model (neglecting EED), and $0.021^{\circ}$ -- $0.067^{\circ}$ for the representative two-spin FAD$^{\bullet -}$/W$_{\mathrm{C}}^{\bullet+}$ system. 
