%
            Figure \ref{fig:eps_vs_Dnu_spread} presents how $\epsilon$ varies across our model grid as a function of $\Delta\nu$, with different panels showing its dependence on age, rotation, metallicity, and mass. Several clear trends emerge from this analysis.

            The age behaviour of $\epsilon$ reveals dependence on evolutionary phases. Pre-MS models generally exhibit a wide spread in $\epsilon$ (1.2--2.2), indicative of rapid structural changes during these phases. As stars approach and settle onto the ZAMS (by $\sim$50 Myr), this spread narrows considerably. Throughout most of the MS, models occupy a well-defined band along which $\epsilon$ slowly increases with increasing age. This band begins with $\epsilon$ values between 1.5 and 1.8 in the early MS and broadens to approximately 1.7--1.9 by 600--700 Myr. Towards TAMS and in the post-MS contraction phase, the $\epsilon$ range expands again (1.6--2.2), indicating renewed structural adjustments following core hydrogen exhaustion. This three-phase pattern in $\epsilon$ is similar to the behaviour observed in $\mathrm{p}_{n_1\ell_0}/\Delta\nu$.

            A particular feature across all parameters is the non-monotonic relationship between $\Delta\nu$ and $\epsilon$. Models with high $\Delta\nu$ values (5--9\,d$^{-1}$) consistently show low $\epsilon$ values (around 1.6), while models with intermediate $\Delta\nu$ values (3--5\,d$^{-1}$) exhibit the highest $\epsilon$ values. Models with the lowest $\Delta\nu$ values ($<$3\,d$^{-1}$) show intermediate $\epsilon$ values, higher than high-$\Delta\nu$ models but lower than intermediate-$\Delta\nu$ models. This pattern creates a characteristic arc in the $\epsilon$--$\Delta\nu$ plane that appears consistently across variations in mass, metallicity, and rotation. The arc corresponds to an underlying temperature sequence, with cooler models occupying the lower-$\Delta\nu$ region on the left and progressively hotter models extending toward higher $\Delta\nu$ values on the right.

            Rotation shows subtle affects on $\epsilon$ values, particularly at lower $\Delta\nu$ values where rapid rotators (with equatorial velocities $\sim$120 km\,s$^{-1}$) predominantly exhibit higher $\epsilon$ values (1.4--2.2) compared to slower rotators ($\sim$30 km\,s$^{-1}$), which show lower values (1.2--2). Non-rotating models display the widest overall spread in $\epsilon$ (1.2--2). This trend is consistent with rotationally induced shifts in pulsation frequencies and phase relations, especially for low-order p-modes \citep{ouazzani_rotational_2012, guo_oscillation_2024}. 

            Metallicity produces a negligible effect on $\epsilon$ during the MS and the post-MS phases. Increasing metallicity systematically decreases $\Delta\nu$, while $\epsilon$ is largely independent of it. This trend is reflected in the pre-MS phase as well, albeit more loosely because of rapid structural changes.

            Stellar mass exhibits a direct relationship with both $\epsilon$ and $\Delta\nu$. During the pre-MS phase, higher-mass models ($M > 2.2\,\mathrm{M}_\odot$) correspond to lower $\Delta\nu$ values with a large spread in $\epsilon$ ($\sim$1.2 to 2.2). Throughout the MS and post-MS contraction phases, higher-mass models exhibit a smaller spread (1.75--2.05) in $\epsilon$ and low $\Delta\nu$ values. 

\section{Conclusions}
\label{sec:conclusions}

We have developed an extensive grid of stellar models for \dsct stars spanning masses from 1.4 to 2.5 M$_\odot$, metallicities from $Z = 0.001$ to 0.026, and rotation rates from zero up to $\Omega/\Omega_{\rm crit} = 0.3$. This work extends prior efforts by incorporating rotation, including higher-degree modes ($\ell \leq 3$), including g and f modes, and tracking stars from the pre-MS through MS to post-MS contraction. 

We have revisited the systematic relationships between pulsation properties and fundamental stellar parameters in \dsct stars. The large frequency separation ($\Delta\nu$) follows a tight power-law relation with mean stellar density, with $\Delta\nu/\Delta\nu_\odot = 0.858 \times (\bar{\rho}/\bar{\rho}_\odot)^{0.507}$. This scaling factor of 0.857 reflects the non-asymptotic nature of the observed p\:modes in \dsct stars, while the power-law exponent closely matches the theoretical expectation of 0.5. We have also established a linear relationship between the fundamental radial mode frequency and the large frequency separation, $\mathrm{p}_{n_1\ell_0} = 3.074\ \Delta\nu + 0.228\,{\rm d}^{-1}$, which holds across our entire parameter space with remarkably low scatter. When rotation is incorporated, the relation becomes $\mathrm{p}_{n_1\ell_0} = 3.018 \ \Delta\nu + 0.497 \ \frac{\Omega}{2\pi} + 0.217\,{\rm d}^{-1}$.
We also analysed the behaviour of the phase offset parameter $\epsilon$ and large frequency separation $\Delta\nu$ across our model grid to see how these vary with fundamental stellar parameters including mass, metallicity, age, and rotation.

The different mode types in our grid exhibit varying penetration depths within stellar interiors, providing complementary sensitivity to the internal density profile at different radial locations. Pressure modes primarily sample the outer envelope and are sensitive to mean density variations, while gravity modes probe deeper regions and are more responsive to core structure and evolutionary changes. The inclusion of rotational effects allows quantification of internal rotation rates through the analysis of rotational splitting patterns, breaking the degeneracy between rotational and evolutionary effects on mode frequencies that has historically limited asteroseismic inference for \dsct stars.

Our modeling of p\:modes, g\:modes, f\:modes, and mixed modes across evolutionary phases yields a significant result: higher-degree, low-order gravity modes in our theoretical models correspond to observed frequency peaks in young $\delta$\,Sct stars. These frequency peaks occur in regions where only g\:modes are theoretically expected, demonstrating that our models are accurately capturing the underlying stellar pulsation physics rather than fortuitously matching observations. This correspondence validates that our grid provides a complete description of the pulsational properties of young $\delta$\,Sct stars, extending beyond the well-established p\:mode regime. These findings establish a new diagnostic avenue: the use of g\:modes to constrain both internal rotation and stellar age, alongside traditional p\:mode asteroseismology.

In a forthcoming paper, we will quantify systematic uncertainties in the inferred stellar parameters that arise from variations in the input physics of our models. This analysis will encompass variations in several physical factors, including convective overshoot, nuclear reaction networks, angular momentum diffusion, and rotational oblateness, among others. This analysis will inform a neural network inference framework for determining stellar parameters from observed \dsct frequencies (Gautam et al. in preparation), building on previous machine learning approaches \citep{scutt_asteroseismology_2023, murphy_grid_2023}. We also plan to use our models to refine the age of the Pleiades cluster, a benchmark open cluster whose precise age is useful for calibrating stellar evolution models. By doing so, we aim to test and validate our method and models. 

