\section{Introduction}
% 段1：////////////////////////////////////
Evaluating partial effects typically involves estimating the effect of a counterfactual change in a (possibly endogenous) target variable\footnote{A \textit{target variable} (also referred to as a \textit{treatment variable}), denoted as $D$, is the variable that a policymaker aims to change. A policymaker hopes to change $D$ to achieve a desired effect on $Y$.} on the various functionals of the distribution of an outcome variable $Y$. %
In the context of continuous treatment, this effect can be quantified using the \textit{unconditional partial effect} ($\mathrm{UPE}$) proposed by \citet{firpo2009} or \citet{rothe2010el}.
%
Formally, we assume that the outcome of interest $Y$ is generated by $Y:=m(D,X,U)$, where $D$ is a continuous endogenous target variable and $X$ is a vector of covariates. We assume that each member of the population experiences an exogenous change in $D$, characterized by $\intven(D,\delta)$ with a smooth function $\intven(\cdot,\cdot)$. For example, $\intven(D,\delta) =D+\delta$ implies
increasing each person's $D$ by a fixed amount and $\intven(D,\delta) =D(1+\delta)$ implies increasing $D$ by a fixed proportion.%
\footnote{Other policy-relevant interventions include
    $\intven(D,\delta) =(D-\mu)\times(1+\sigma\delta)+\mu$,
    where $\mu:=E[D]$; $\sigma\in [-1,1]$ is a free parameter. A positive (or negative) $\sigma$ raises (or shrinks) the dispersion (inequality) of $D$ while maintaining its mean constant.}
Then for a functional $\phi$ of $F_Y$, the \textit{Partial Effect} ($\mathrm{UPE}$) associated with
$\intven(\cdot,\cdot)$ is defined as
\begin{flalign*}
    \mathrm{UPE}
    :=\lim\limits_{\delta\to 0}\frac{\phi(F_{Y_\delta})-\phi(F_{Y})}{\delta}
\end{flalign*}
where $Y_\delta=m(\intven(D,\delta),X,U)$ denotes the counterfactual outcome. Numerous  policy effects---including the  distribution effect ($\phi = \text{identity mapping}$),  quantile effect ($\phi = \inf\{y:F_\cdot(y) \geq \tau \}$ for any $\tau \in (0,1)$), and  mean effect ($\phi = \int y \,dF_{\cdot}(y)$)---can be considered special cases of this general formulation. Furthermore, general policy intervention $\intven(\cdot,\cdot)$ (\citet{mms2024}) encompasses \textit{location shift} $\intven(D,\delta) = D+\delta$  as a special case. The latter is studied by \citet{firpo2009} and \citet{rothe2010el}.

When $D$ is endogenous, the identification of $\mathrm{UPE}$ generally requires using an instrumental variable.
However, in practice, finding a valid instrument may not be possible. In this study, we demonstrate that a local version of $\mathrm{UPE}$ is identifiable even if the instrument is not accessible. We exploit the sharp \textit{regression kink (RK) design}, where (i) $D$ is generated by an endogenous running variable $X$ through a deterministic function $b:\R\to\R$, such that $D=b(X)$; (ii) $b(\cdot)$ is continuous but kinked at a known threshold $x_0$---that is, $\lim_{x\downarrow x_0} b'(x)\ne \lim_{x\uparrow x_0}b'(x)$. The RK design was first studied by \citet{card2015ecta}. To motivate RK design's use, let us consider the following example.
\\
\textit{Example}.
In most countries and regions, UI benefits $D$ are set as $D=\alpha\min\{X, x_0\}$, where $X$ is the previous wage and $0<\alpha <1$ is a proportion dependent on the UI policies. In practice, evaluating the causal effect of UI benefits $D$ on unemployment durations $Y$ is difficult because any benefit-related variable is likely to be correlated with the unobserved characteristics $U$ of individuals that affect both $X$ and $Y$; that is, an instrumental variable for $D$ that is independent of $U$ is unlikely to exist.

In the RK design, we focus on the \textit{Partial Effect at the threshold $x_0$} ($\upe$) with a general intervention $\intven(\cdot,\cdot)$.
This quantity is defined as follows:
\begin{flalign*}
    \upe
    :=\lim\limits_{\delta\to 0}\frac{\phi(F_{Y_\delta|X=x_0})-\phi(F_{Y|X=x_0})}{\delta},
\end{flalign*}
where $Y_\delta:=m(\intven(b(X),\delta),X,U)$ denotes the counterfactual outcome. %

Two points are worth noting here.
First, $\upe$ can be considered a generalization of the ``local average treatment effect'' $E[\dev{D}{m}(b(x_0),x_0,U)|X=x_0]$ proposed by \citet{card2015ecta}: When functional $\phi$ is set to $E[\cdot|X=x_0]:=\int y \,dF_{\cdot|X}(y|x_0)$, $\upe$ becomes the \textit{Mean Partial Effect} ($\umpe$):
\begin{flalign*}
    \umpe
    :=\lim\limits_{\delta\to 0} \frac{E(Y_{\delta}|\,X=x_0) - E(Y|\,X=x_0)}{\delta}
    = \kappa_0 E\left[\dev{D}{m}(b(x_0),x_0,U) |\,X=x_0 \right],
\end{flalign*}
where $\kappa_0:=\dev{\delta}{\intven}(b(x_0),\delta)|_{\delta=0}$, a constant. Thus, $\umpe$ is equivalent to this ``local average treatment effect'' when location shift $\intven(D,\delta)=D+\delta$ is adopted. %

Second, intervention $\intven(b(X),\delta)$ is easily implementable in the sharp RK design. As intervention function $\intven(\cdot,\cdot)$ is selected by the policymaker and the form of assignment rule $b(\cdot)$ is also known, changing $b(X)$ to $\intven(b(X),\delta)$ amounts to changing either the location or the shape of $b(\cdot)$, or both, depending on the form of $\intven(\cdot,\cdot)$. We illustrate this point using the above-given example. \\
\textit{Example(continued)}:
In Figure \ref{fig:value.inter}(a), each unemployed individual receives an additional $\delta$ units of benefits. In Figure \ref{fig:value.inter}(b), the benefits received by unemployed individuals with previous wages below $x_0$ are reduced by a fixed proportion, whereas the benefits of those with previous wages above $x_0$ are reduced by $\delta$ units. %
