% Contributions ////////////////////////
\textit{Our study makes three contributions to the literature}. %
First, we generalize the  policy effect analysis from the context of conditional exogeneity (\citet{firpo2009}, \citet{rothe2010el,rothe2012}, \citet{mms2024}) to the RK design by proposing a new class of causal quantities, referred to as the \textit{Partial Effect at the threshold} ($\upe$).
We provide a unified framework for identifying, estimating, and inferring $\upe$ associated with a general intervention.
Our novel identification strategy can be divided into two steps.
First, we identify the \textit{distribution partial effect at the threshold} ($\udpe$). %
Second, based on the identified $\udpe$, we establish a relationship between $\upe$ and $\udpe$ for any \textit{Hadamard-differentiable} functional, which can thus identify $\upe$. %

%
Second, we provide a a unifying formula that clarifies the causal interpretations of numerous sharp RK estimands from the literature.
Our general identification formula can be used to quantify the effect of an infinitesimal change in a target variable on various functionals of $F_{Y|X=x_0}$, including its moments, quantiles, and inequality measures, such as the interquartile range and coefficient of variation. For the location shift, we prove that the sharp RK estimand defined by \citet{card2015ecta} identifies $\umpe$ and the quantile RK $(\rkd{Q})$ estimand---defined by \citet{cs2019}---identifies the \textit{quantile partial effect at the threshold} ($\uqpe$):
\begin{flalign*}
    \uqpe(\tau):=\lim_{\delta \to 0} \frac{Q_{Y_\delta|X}(\tau|x_0)-Q_{Y|X}(\tau|x_0)}{\delta},
\end{flalign*}
where $Q_{\cdot|X}(\tau|x_0):=\inf\{y: F_{\cdot|X}(y|x_0)\geq \tau\}$ is the conditional quantile function. We note that in the absence of the condition of continuous $f_{Y|X}(\cdot|\cdot)$, $\rkd{Q}(\tau)$ identifies a weighted average of structural derivatives for the subpopulation of individuals with outcome $Y=Q_{Y|X}(\tau|x_0)$ at kink $x_0$---the primary conclusion of \citet{cs2019}.

% kappa0
Third, we demonstrate that $\upe$ associated with general intervention $D_\delta=\intven(D,\delta)$ is exactly $\kappa_0$ times that associated with location shift $D_\delta=D+\delta$. Our empirical application illustrates this result's implication. In analyzing the causal effects of UI benefits on unemployment durations, the magnitude of the partial effect changes tremendously with different forms of policy intervention.


% estimation and inference
Additionally, we develop the asymptotic theory and multiplier bootstrap for $\upe$ based on the local polynomial regression estimator. Practical guidelines for estimation and inference are provided.

\textit{Related literature}:
This study is connected to the literature on the identification and inference of the policy effect.
\citet{firpo2009} study $\mathrm{UQPE}$ under unconfoundedness and develop the recentered influence function regression method to estimate $\mathrm{UQPE}$. \citet{rothe2010el} considers the identification of $\mathrm{UPE}$ with endogenous $D$ using instrumental variables (the control variable approach proposed by \citet{imbensNewey2009}). %
\citet{mms2024} generalize $\mathrm{UQPE}$ to the case of general policy intervention $D_\delta=\intven(D,\delta)$. However, their analysis relies heavily on the unconfoundedness assumption. The following table summarizes the counterfactual models and parameters used in the aforementioned studies. To the best of our knowledge, our study is the first to identify and estimate  policy effects in the RK design.
