\section{Simulation studies} \label{sec:MC}
In this section, we conduct the Monte Carlo simulations to assess the finite sample performances of the estimators proposed in Section \ref{sec:stats.esti}.
%-----------------------------------
\subsection{Setup}
%-----------------------------------
We consider the following data generating process:
\begin{flalign*}
    Y&= 6 - 3 D + 2 X + X^2 + 1.5 D X^2 + (1+D+X)U,\\
    D&= \min\{X+1,1\},
\end{flalign*}
where the assignment rule of $D$ has a structure similar to that in the subsequent empirical application.
Then, we suppose the following three policy interventions:
\begin{flalign}
    \intven(D,\delta) &= D + \delta  \implies \kappa_0 = 1, \tag{I} \label{simu:intven1}\\
    \intven(D,\delta) &= \frac{D}{1+2\delta} \implies \kappa_0= -2, \tag{II} \label{simu:intven2}\\
    \intven(D,\delta) &= D(1+\delta)-0.5\delta \implies \kappa_0= 0.5, \tag{III} \label{simu:intven3}
\end{flalign}
where intervention (\ref{simu:intven1}) represents a common location shift; intervention (\ref{simu:intven2}) is a kind of scale shift; intervention (\ref{simu:intven3}) considers both location and scale changes.
Moreover, we assume that the data for $(X,U)$ are generated according to:
\begin{flalign}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \,X\, \\
        \,U\, \\
    \end{pmatrix}
    \overset{i.i.d}{\sim}\;\mathcal{N}
    \begin{pmatrix}
        \begin{pmatrix}
            \, 0 \, \\
            \, 0 \,
        \end{pmatrix}\, , \,
        \begin{pmatrix}
            \sigma_{X}^{2} & \rho_{X,U} \sigma_{X} \sigma_{U} \\
            \rho_{X,U} \sigma_{U} \sigma_{X} & \sigma_{U}^2  \\
        \end{pmatrix}
    \end{pmatrix}, \notag
\end{flalign}
where $\sigma_{X} = 0.1781742$ and $\rho_{X,U} = 0.25$ are selected to match those of \citet{cct2014} and $\sigma_{U} = 10\sigma_{X}$.

We simulate the behavior of the estimators of $\udpe$, $\uqpe$, $\umpe$, $\iqrpe$, and $\cvpe$ based on Algorithm \ref{alg:infer}, where the heterogeneous effects $\udpe(y_\tau(x_0))$ and $\uqpe(\tau)$ are estimated over the grid $\tau = (0.1,0.075,\ldots,0.9)$.
In the experiment, we use sample sizes $N = \{500, 2000\}$ for comparison and set the number of Monte Carlo replications to $2,500$. For each replication, we use the multiplier bootstrap in Algorithm \ref{alg:infer} with $1000$ repetitions to obtain 90\% uniform confidence bands for the functionals of interest. In each local polynomial regression, $K(\cdot)$ is set to triangular kernel $K(u)=(1-|u|)\I{|u|\leq 1}$, and the polynomial order is set to $p = 2$.

