\section{Empirical applications} \label{sec:empirical}
In this section, we use data from the Continuous Wage and Benefit History Project (CWBH) for Louisiana to estimate the effect of the UI benefits $D$ on the unemployment durations $Y$ at the kink. Following \citet{landais2015} and \citet{cs2019}, $Y$ is measured by the number of weeks of UI claims (UI Claimed) or the actual paid number of weeks (UI Paid). The target variable $D$ is computed using insurance rule $D = \min\{\alpha X,d_{\max}\}$ for some $\alpha\in(0,1)$, where running variable $X$ is the highest quarter wage in the base period.

We use data between September 1982 and December 1983, during which $\alpha=0.04$,
kink $x_0 = \$5125$ and $d_{\max}=\$205$.\footnote{Figure 2 in \citet{landais2015} depicts the weekly benefit amount schedule in Louisiana for different periods.} We refer interested readers to \citet{landais2015} for descriptive statistics as well as for further details regarding the data.

We estimate $\udpe$ and $\uqpe$ to discover heterogeneity, $\iqrpe$ and $\cvpe$ to detect inequality in the sample.
Policy intervention assumes the form of location shift $\intven(D,\delta) = D+\delta$. All estimation and inference procedures follow Algorithm \ref{alg:infer}, where we set grids $\mathcal{T} = \{0.1,0.10625,\ldots,0.9\}$ with 129 points to simulate the limit processes and set the number of multiplier bootstrap resampling to 5,000. The order of the local polynomial regression is set to $p = 2$ considering boundary bias.

