For $\udpe$, the following patterns are obtained: First, the estimates are negative for all points.
Second, the proportion of workers with unemployment durations in the (0.10, 0.25) and (0.75, 0.90) quantile intervals decreases, while the proportion in the interquartile interval increases. Notably, for $0 < \tau_1 < \tau_2 < 1 $, an infinitesimal change in the proportion of $Y\in (y_{\tau_1}(x_0),y_{\tau_2}(x_0))$ is given by
\begin{flalign*}
    &\partial P_Y(\tau_1,\tau_2)\\
    &=\lim_{\delta\to 0}\frac{1}{\delta}\bigg\{ P\big[y_{\tau_1}(x_0)< Y_\delta \leq y_{\tau_2}(x_0)|X=x_0\big].
    -P\big[y_{\tau_1}(x_0)< Y \leq y_{\tau_2}(x_0)|X=x_0\big]\bigg\}\\
    &=\lim_{\delta\to 0}\bigg\{\frac{F_{Y_\delta}(y_{\tau_2}(x_0)|x_0)-F_{Y_\delta}(y_{\tau_1}(x_0)|x_0)}{\delta}-\frac{F_{Y}(y_{\tau_2}(x_0)|x_0)-F_{Y}(y_{\tau_1}(x_0)|x_0)}{\delta}\bigg\}\\
    &=\lim_{\delta\to 0}\bigg\{\frac{F_{Y_\delta}(y_{\tau_2}(x_0)|x_0)-F_{Y}(y_{\tau_2}(x_0)|x_0)}{\delta}-\frac{F_{Y_\delta}(y_{\tau_1}(x_0)|x_0)-F_{Y}(y_{\tau_1}(x_0)|x_0)}{\delta}\bigg\}\\
    &= \udpe(y_{\tau_2}(x_0))-\udpe(y_{\tau_1}(x_0)).
\end{flalign*}
Hence, in panel A, we find that $\widehat{\partial P_Y}(0.10, 0.25)$, $\widehat{\partial P_Y}(0.75, 0.90) < 0$ and $\widehat{\partial P_Y}(0.25, 0.75) > 0$ irrespective of the measure of $Y$ used.
This indicates the second pattern.
Third, the uniform nullity test indicates that $\udpe$s significantly differ from zero. Fourth, the homogeneity test results suggest that they are significantly heterogeneous.

% //////////////
For $\uqpe$, %
first, the signs of the estimates are positive at all quantiles, which can also be deduced from the negative signs of $\udpe$ estimates because $\widehat{f}_{Y|X}(y_\tau(x_0)|x_0) > 0 $ for all considered $\tau$. %
Second, the estimates of $\uqpe$ are relatively high in the middle and high quantiles ($\tau \in (0.45, 0.6)\cup\{0.9\}$). %
Third, the uniform nullity test reveals that $\uqpe$s significantly differ from zero.
Fourth, the homogeneity test indicates that they also exhibit significant heterogeneity.
These patterns are consistent with those of \citet{cs2019}, who use the estimator of $\rkd{Q}$.

