\section{Summary} \label{sec:summary}

This study generalizes the  policy effect analysis to the context of regression kink design by proposing a new class of causal quantities, referred to as the \textit{Partial Effect at the threshold} ($\upe$).
We provide a unified framework for identifying, estimating, and inferring $\upe$ associated with a general policy intervention. Our study is the first to discover and clarify the connections between numerous RK estimands in the literature and provide a unifying formula to understand the causal interpretation of various RK estimands.

We develop the asymptotic theory and multiplier bootstrap for these  partial effects. Practical guidelines for estimation and
inference are provided. We apply the method to analyze the causal effects of the UI benefits on unemployment durations.
We find that although a higher UI benefit causes longer unemployment
durations, the dispersion of durations tends to shrink simultaneously. Moreover,
the magnitude of the partial effect may change substantially  by adopting interventions other than simple location shifts.


