\section{Substrate modification networks} \label{sec:substrate_modification_network}


Certain reaction archetypes or motifs appear repeatedly in biochemical reaction networks. 
One common motif type is {\em transformation}, wherein a substrate $S$ is transformed to another substrate $S^*$, typically depicted as a {\em reaction} $S \to S^*$. 
Networks with such reactions are ubiquitous and include activation-inactivation cycles \cite{goldbeter1981amplified}, cascades \cite{huang1996ultrasensitivity,ferrell1996tripping}, chemotaxis \cite{barkai1997robustness}, signaling \cite{shinar2007input}, metabolic regulation \cite{martinov2000substrate}, gene regulation -- natural \cite{reinitz1990theoretical} and engineered \cite{gardner2000construction}. 


Another common biochemical motif is a {\em transfer} reaction, which involves two substrates on the reactant side and two different substrates on the product side. 
For example, in a phosphate transfer reaction, a phosphate group is transferred from a donor $X_p$ to an acceptor $Y$: 
\begin{align} \label{eq:transfer}
X_p + Y~  \longrightarrow   ~X + Y_p. 
\end{align}


Transformation reaction $S \to S^*$ and transfer reaction $X + Y^* \to X^* + Y$ are only two types of composite reactions with the same number of substrates on the reactant and the product side, one-one and two-two respectively. 
We give here a complete list of all motif types that involve at most two substrates on the reactant side and at most two substrates on the product side:  
(a) transformation: $S \to S^*$,
(b) transfer: $X + Y^* \to X^* + Y$,
(c) cleaving: $X \to Y + Y^*$, 
(d) binding: $X + X^* \to Y$, 
(e) inflow: $0 \to S$, 
(f) outflow: $S \to 0$, 
(g) sequestration: $S + S^* \to 0$, and 
(h) pair production: $0 \to S + S^*$. 
Sometimes the product or reactant species may not be distinct, resulting in higher stoichiometric coefficients. 
For example, the reaction $X + X \to Y$ (or $2X \to Y$) is allowed as a binding reaction. 

Consider {\em glycolysis}, as an illustration of the ubiquitousness of these reaction motifs.  
The overall reaction is a cleaving reaction: 
\[
\rm{glucose} \longrightarrow  \rm{pyruvate} + \rm{pyruvate}. 
\]
When resolved at a higher detail, the standard depiction of glycolysis has 10 steps \cite{alberts2017molecular} with each step involving multiple reactions. Every reaction within each step is one of the following {\em types}: 
(a) transformation reaction $S \to S'$ (steps 2, 5, 8), 
(b) transfer reaction $S_1 + S_2 \to S_1' + S_2'$ (steps 1, 3, 6, 7, 10), 
(c) cleaving reaction $S \to S_1' + S_2'$ (steps 4, 9), 
(d) binding reaction $S_1 + S_2 \to S'$ (step 4). 


Most biochemical reactions involve at most two substrates on either side, and therefore this is our focus. 
However, we do allow for higher number of substrates in the mathematical results of this paper. 
All these reactions, collectively are called {\em substrate modification reactions}, and a collection of such reactions is called a {\em substrate modification network}. 


\section{Detailed models of a substrate modification network} \label{sec:detailed_model}

A {\em detailed model} goes further than describing the identity of the initial substrates and the product substrates because it also specifies the nature of the enzyme action and intervening reaction steps. 
A common detailed model of the transformation reaction $S \to S^*$ uses the Henri-Michaelis-Menten mechanism \cite{johnson2011original,punekar2018henri}
\begin{align} \label{eq:rxnsteps_main}
S + E~  \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~C \longrightarrow S^* + E,
\end{align}
where $E$ is an enzyme that facilitates the reaction while $C$ is an intermediate compound, often denoted as $SE$ to indicate that it is formed from the bonding of $S$ and $E$. 
Numerous other detailed mechanisms for $S \to S^*$ have been proposed, we illustrate with just two variations: 

\begin{align} \label{eq:rxnsteps6_main}
S + E~  \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~&C_1 \longrightarrow ~C_2 \longrightarrow S^* + E. \\
S + E~  \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~&C \longrightarrow  C' ~  \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~ S^* + E. 
\end{align}
Intricate features of product formation curves can be fitted with detailed models with more complex topology and a larger number of intermediate compounds. 


In order to distinguish modeling hierarchies, we refer to $S \to S^*$ as a {\em composite reaction}; the individual reaction networks appearing in \eqref{eq:rxnsteps_main}-\eqref{eq:rxnsteps6_main} as its  {\em detailed model}, and the reactions in each detailed model as {\em reaction steps} for that detailed model.  
We wish to allow any reasonable detailed model in this paper. 
Precise mathematical definitions and more examples of detailed models can be found in Section S1.4 in the supplementary materials. 
An example of an {\em invalid} detailed mechanism for $S \to S^*$ is the so-called reversible Michaelis-Menten scheme  
\begin{align} \label{eq:revMM_main}
S + E~  \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~C \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~S^* + E. 
\end{align}
This reaction does not conclude with all of $S$ being consumed and transformed to $S^*$, but rather equilibrates between $S$ and $S^*$.  
In fact, due to the symmetry between their roles, the labeling of one substrate as reactant and another as product is arbitrary and based on initial values rather than the underlying reaction mechanism. 



\section{Skeleton of a substrate modification network} \label{sec:skeleton_hypergraph}

A skeleton of a substrate modification network is a {\em hypergraph} which tracks the substrate modifications while ignoring details about the enzymes and substrate-enzyme compounds. 
Essentially, a skeleton hides the detailed model and only conveys information on the underlying composite substrate modification reactions. 

We first define a hypergraph as an abstract object after which we proceed to define a skeleton as a certain directed hypergraph. 
A (directed) hypergraph extends the notion of a (directed) graph -- as such it has nodes and (directed) hyperedges. 
A `directed edge' in a `graph' connects one tail node to a head node. 
A `directed hyperedge' in a `hypergraph' connects a nonempty set of tail nodes to a nonempty set of head nodes. 
In a hypergraph representation of a substrate modification network, the set of nodes is the set of substrates, and may additionally include the node $0$. 
For every reaction, there is a hyperedge with the reactant substrates acting as the set of tail nodes while the product substrates acting as the set of head nodes. 
Occasionally, a substrate may have stoichiometry that is 2 or higher. 
In such a case, the substrate is `doubled up', so that the set of tail nodes and the set of head nodes should be more accurately viewed as multisets. 
A {\em multiset} is simply an extension of a set where some element might appear multiple times. 


We will use the ``{\em split arrow}'' representation to draw a hypergraph skeleton. 
When a composite reaction is a transformation reaction $S \to S^*$, it appears unchanged in the skeleton. 
An inflow reaction or outflow reaction also appears unchanged in a skeleton. 
