In the following sections, we provide precise mathematical definitions, notations, theorems which support our three main results on existence of positive steady state, concentration robustness (of a complex), and ACR, together with their corresponding proofs. 

\section{Substrate modification networks}\label{sec:submodnetwork}

The main purpose of this section is to recall relevant definitions arising from substrate modification networks and set up notations which are used in later sections. The section is structured as follows. In Section \ref{sec:skeleton} and \ref{sec:current_rep}, we recall skeletons and currents. Next, in Section \ref{sec:reactionnetwork}, we present some necessary terminologies from reaction network theory in order to describe different types of detailed models in Section \ref{sec:detailedmodel}. Lastly, in Section \ref{sec:enzyme}, we provide some convenient notations to specify the enzyme acting in each composite reaction. 
% These detailed models will lead to (mass-action) ODE systems which we will analyze in Sections \ref{sec:HMM} and \ref{sec:general}.

\subsection{Skeleton}\label{sec:skeleton}

We consider a substrate modification network with $n$ substrates $S_1, S_2,\dots, S_n$. For the sake of convenience and consistency, we also introduce the ``null substrate" $S_0$ to represent the zero complex which appears in inflow and outflow reactions. For example, the inflow reaction $0\to S_1$ will be written interchangebly as $S_0\to S_1.$

The \textit{skeleton} of the substrate modification network is a (directed) hypergraph, which is denoted by $G=(V,R)$. 
% Intuitively, a hypergraph is a generalization of a graph in which an edge  connects one nonempty set of vertices to another nonempty set of vertices. 
The set of vertices is $V=\{S_0, S_1, \dots, S_n\}$ ($S_0$ is omitted if the network does not contain the $0$ complex). The set of hyperedges represents the composite reactions in the substrate modification network. For each hyperedge $\ell\in R$, let $T_\ell$ and $H_\ell$ be the set of indices of the tail vertices and head vertices. Each hyperedge $\ell$ represents a composite reaction 
\[
\sum_{i\in T_\ell} S_i \to \sum_{i\in H_\ell} S_i.
\]    



\begin{example}
    Consider a substrate modification network with the following composite reactions
    \[
    S_0\to S_1,\quad S_1+S_2\to S_3+S_4, \quad S_4\to S_1+ S_3, \quad S_3\to S_2
    \]
    Then the skeleton of the substrate modification network is a hypergraph $G=(V,R)$ where 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $V=\{S_0,S_1,S_2,S_3,S_4\}$ and
        \item  $R=\{(\{S_0\},\{S_1\}), (\{S_1,S_2\},\{S_3,S_4\}), (\{S_4\},\{S_1,S_3\}), (\{S_3\},\{S_2\})\}$.
    \end{itemize}

\end{example}

Furthermore, we allow the set of tail and head vertices (and correspondingly $T_\ell$ and $H_\ell$) to be multisets, where there can be repeated instances of their elements. This enables us to represent reactions where the stoichiometric coefficient of a substrate is greater than one. For example, the reaction $S_1\to 2S_2$ can be represented by the hyperedge $(\{S_1\}, \{S_2,S_2\})$.

% When the stoichiometric coefficients in all reactions are at most 1 (and thus $T_\ell$ and $H_\ell$ are sets instead of multisets), we can illustrate a hyperedge by drawing a normal arrow if there is only one head and one tail vertex, and a \textit{split arrow} if there are multiple head vertices or multiple tail vertices (see Figure \ref{fig:splitedges2_2}).


\subsection{Current}\label{sec:current_rep}
\begin{definition}
Consider a substrate modification network and its skeleton $G=(V,R)$. In a \textit{current assignment}, we associate each edge $\ell\in R$ with a non-negative quantity $I_\ell$ (called \textit{current}) which satisfy the following balance property. For each vertex $S_i$  (excluding $S_0$) we must have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:vertexbalance}
\sum_{\ell:i\in T_\ell} I_\ell = \sum_{\ell: i\in H_\ell } I_\ell
\end{equation}
% We call equation \eqref{eq:vertexbalance} a \textit{vertex-balance condition}. 
In addition, a \textit{positive current assignment} is a current assignment in which $I_\ell>0$ for all $\ell\in R$.   
\end{definition}


% It is easy to see that a trivial choice of currents is $I_\ell=0$ for all $\ell\in R$. However, since we only discuss positive steady states in later sections, we are only interested in the case when there exists a choice of \underline{positive currents} on the skeleton.

\begin{definition}
We say that a pair of hyperedges $(\ell,m)$ are \textit{current-balanced} if there is a positive current assignment and for any choice of positive currents, we have $I_\ell = I_{m}$.  
\end{definition}

