A common detailed model of the transformation reaction $S \to S^*$ uses the Henri-Michaelis-Menten mechanism \cite{johnson2011original,punekar2018henri}
\begin{align} \label{eq:rxnsteps}
S + E~  \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~C \longrightarrow S^* + E,
\end{align}
where $E$ is an enzyme that facilitates the reaction while $C$ is an intermediate compound, often denoted as $SE$ to indicate that it is formed from the bonding of $S$ and $E$.  
A detailed model may have more than one intermediate compound, going through a series of steps before the enzyme and the product are released. 
The simplest instance of this has two compounds and two irreversible reactions leading to the final product release
\begin{align} \label{eq:rxnsteps6}
S + E~  \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~C_1 \longrightarrow ~C_2 \longrightarrow S^* + E. 
% S + E~  \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~C_1 \xrightleftarrows{}{} ~C_2 \longrightarrow S^* + E, \mbox{ (reversible intermediate step). } 
\end{align}

The nature of the detailed model is relevant when describing the dynamics, even if one is only interested in the dynamics of the substrates involved. 
In \cite{berezhkovskii2017dependence}, different detailed models that each assume two types of intermediate compound ($C_1$ and $C_2$), as shown in  \eqref{eq:rxnsteps_altmix}, make different predictions for the rates of product formation. 

