\section{Heterogenous conservation laws}\label{sec:appl}

In this final section, we apply Theorem \ref{thm-1} to quantify 
the Sobolev regularity of entropy solutions 
to heterogeneous scalar conservation laws with 
a nonlinear flux and general initial data $u_0\in L^\infty$. 
This represents the first such regularity 
result for multidimensional 
equations with spatial heterogeneity. 
A substantial body of literature addresses 
the regularizing effect in homogeneous conservation 
laws; an exhaustive review is beyond our scope. 
In addition to references cited in the introduction 
(notably \cite{Lions:1994qy}), we refer the reader to 
\cite{Gess:2019ab,Golse:2013aa,Jabin:2002aa,
Jabin:2010aa,Lellis:2003aa,Tadmor:2006vn}, 
the books \cite{Dafermos:2005mz,Perthame:2002qy}, and 
discussions therein. However, these works all 
consider flux functions independent 
of the spatial variable $\mx$.

In recent years, many researchers have dedicated 
significant effort to analyzing conservation laws with 
$\mx$-dependent flux functions. This effort has been partly 
motivated by numerous applications, often involving discontinuous 
$\mx$-dependence and restricted to one spatial dimension 
(see, e.g., \cite{Andreianov:2010fk} 
and references therein). Most of these studies 
focus on existence, uniqueness of 
solutions and convergence properties 
of numerical schemes. However, the problem of 
deriving quantitative compactness or regularity estimates 
for solutions originating from general initial data $u_0 \in L^\infty$ 
(and a nonlinear flux) remained open.  
We are only aware of the recent work \cite{Karlsen:2023ae}, which 
addressed a specific one-dimensional conservation law with 
nonlinear flux $f(k(x),u)$, where $k$ is a spatial coefficient 
and $u_0\in L^\infty$. The authors constructed solutions 
that belong to a Besov space.

Hereafter, we adopt the notations and well-posedness 
framework established in \cite{Dalibard:2006ab}, which 
addresses scalar conservation laws of the form
\begin{equation}\label{scl-heter}
	\pa_t u(t,\mx)+\sum_{j=1}^d \pa_{x_j} A_j(\mx,u(t,\mx))=0, 
	\qquad u|_{t=0}=u_0\in (L^1\cap L^\infty)(\R^d),
\end{equation} 
where the flux $A=(A_1,\dots,A_d):\R^{d+1}\to \R^d$ is such that
\begin{itemize}
	\item $a_j(\mx,\lambda):=\pa_\lambda A_j(\mx,\lambda)
	\in C^{1}(\R^{d+1})$ and we denote $a=\pa_\lambda  A$;

	\item $a_{d+1}(\mx,\lambda):=-\sum\limits_{j=1}^d
	\pa_{x_j}A_j(\mx,\lambda)\in L^\infty(\R^{d+1})$;

	\item $a_{d+1}(\mx,0)=0$.
\end{itemize} 

\begin{remark}
It is worth noting that a more general scenario---particularly 
regarding the condition $a_{d+1}(\mx,0)=0$---can be 
investigated. However, the refinement of such conditions 
and the optimization of the spatial regularity of 
the flux vector $A$ (see Remark \ref{rem:mff_assmpt_x}) 
will be pursued elsewhere.
\end{remark}

In order for \eqref{scl-heter} to exhibit 
a quantitative regularising effect, it is necessary for the 
flux to satisfy a nonlinearity condition. 
Within the framework of the kinetic formulation (see below), this 
corresponds to the non-degeneracy condition \eqref{non-deg}, 
which, for a fixed $K \subset\subset \R^d$ 
and $L \subset \subset \R$, here takes the form
\begin{align}\label{non-deg-scl}
	\sup\limits_{\mx\in K}
	\sup\limits_{(\xi_0,\xi_1,\ldots,\xi_d)\in \bS^d} 
	\meas\seq{\lambda\in L: 
	\abs{\xi_0+\sum\limits_{k=1}^d
	\xi_k a_k(\mx,\lambda)}<\nu}\leq \nu^{\alpha},
\end{align} for some fixed $\alpha>0$ and any $\nu>0$.

By \cite[Theorem 3]{Dalibard:2006ab}, we 
know that the function
$$
\chi(t,\mx,\lambda)=
\begin{cases}
	1, & {\rm if} \ \ 0\leq \lambda <u(t,\mx), \\
	-1, & {\rm if} \ \ u(t,\mx) \leq \lambda <0, \\
	0, & {\rm otherwise},
\end{cases}
$$ 
satisfies the following kinetic equation:
\begin{equation*}%\label{kinetic}
	\pa_t \chi + \Div_{\mx}(a(\mx,\lambda)\chi)
	+\pa_\lambda (a_{d+1}(\mx,\lambda)\chi)
	=\pa_\lambda m,
\end{equation*} 
where $m$ is a non-negative measure 
from $\cM_{\loc}((0,\infty)\times \R^{d+1})$. 

Therefore, according to Theorem \ref{thm-1}, 
it follows that for any $\rho \in C_c^1(\R)$, 
\begin{equation}\label{reg-scl}
	\int_{\R}\chi(t,\mx,\lambda)
	\rho(\lambda)\, d\lambda \in 
	W_{\loc}^{\beta,r}((0,\infty)\times \R^d)
\end{equation} 
for some $\beta>0$ and $r>1$. 

Assuming the entropy solution $u$ is bounded by a 
constant $M$, and selecting $\rho \in C^1(\R)$ such 
that $\rho = 1$ for $\lambda \in [-M, M]$, it 
follows from \eqref{reg-scl} that
$$
\int_{\R} \chi(t,\mx,\lambda)\rho(\lambda)
\, d\lambda=u(t,\mx) 
\in W_{\loc}^{\beta,r}((0,\infty)\times \R^d),
$$ 
thus obtaining the fractional 
Sobolev regularity of the entropy solution $u$ to 
the heterogenous conservation 
law \eqref{scl-heter}, under 
the nonlinearity condition \eqref{non-deg-scl}.


