\title{
Acoustoelectric superlattices
}
\author{Eli Meril}
\author{Unmesh Ghorai}
\author{Tobias Holder}
\author{Rafi Bistritzer}
\email{rafib@tauex.tau.ac.il}
\affiliation{School of Physics and Astronomy, Tel Aviv University, Tel Aviv 69978, Israel}
\date{\today}

\begin{abstract}

We introduce a new class of tunable periodic structures, 
formed by launching two obliquely propagating surface acoustic waves on a piezoelectric substrate that supports a two-dimensional quantum material. The resulting \emph{acoustoelectric superlattice} exhibits two salient features. First, its periodicity is widely tunable, spanning a length scale intermediate between moiré superlattices and optical lattices, enabling the formation of narrow, topologically nontrivial energy bands. Second, unlike moiré systems, where the superlattice amplitude is set by intrinsic interlayer tunneling and lattice relaxation, the amplitude of the acoustoelectric potential is externally tunable via the surface acoustic wave power. Using massive monolayer graphene as an example, we demonstrate that varying the frequencies and power of the surface acoustic waves enables \emph{in-situ} control over the band structure of the 2D material, generating flat bands and nontrivial valley Chern numbers, featuring a highly localized Berry curvature. 

\end{abstract}
\maketitle

\emph{Introduction---}
Controlling the periodicity of a material is a powerful means of shaping its physical behavior. Over the years, extensive efforts have been devoted to engineering artificial periodic structures with tunable and often unconventional properties.
Prominent examples include photonic \cite{photonicCystalsReview2019} and phononic crystals \cite{sonicMaterials2000, phononicCrystalsReview2010}, which control electromagnetic and mechanical wave propagation at the micron scale; optical lattices\cite{coldAtomLatticesReview2007}, where ultracold atoms are confined in tunable periodic potentials to emulate strongly correlated systems; and metamaterials\cite{3Dmetamaterials2019}, which are structured at subwavelength scales to exhibit unconventional electromagnetic responses. Among these, moiré superlattices, formed by stacking and twisting layers of two-dimensional (2D) materials, have emerged as a particularly fruitful platform~\cite{moireBands2011, 
moireButterflies_2011, 
MATBG_Cao_Mott_2018, 
MATBG_Cao_SC_2018, 
MATBG_Sharpe_FM_2019, 
MATBG_DiracRivavls_Zondiner_2020, 
twistedCrI3_Song_2021, 
MATBG_Pomeranchuk_Rozen_2021, 
TBG_Review_Allan_Eva_2020, 
TBG_PhysToday_2024}. These structures realize tunable periodicities on the order of 10nm, giving rise to flat and topologically nontrivial bands. Moiré systems have thus opened new frontiers in condensed matter physics, enabling the exploration of strong correlations \cite{ 
MATBG_CI_Xie_2019, 
MATBG_CI_Nuckolls_2020, 
MATBG_CI_Stepanov_2021, 
MATBG_CI_Park_2023, 
MATBG_Felectrons_Song_2022, 
MATBG_CI_Wang_2024}, superconductivity 
\cite{
MATBG_Cao_SC_2018, 
MATBG_SC_Yankowitz_2019, 
MATG_SC_Stepanov_2020, 
MATG_SC_Hao_2021, 
MATG_SC_Park_2021}, 
quantum geometry \cite{Hastings2021,
Toermae2022,Toermae2023,Yu2025,Verma2025}, and magnetic phases~\cite{
MATBG_Top_Serlin_2020,  
MATBG_Top_Li_2021, 
MATBG_Top_Tseng_2022, 
MATBG_Top_Kuiri_2022}
in previously inaccessible regimes.


In this work, we propose and theoretically investigate a new class of periodic structures: the acoustoelectric superlattice (ASL). As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig: ASL}a, an ASL is formed by placing a 2D material on a piezoelectric substrate through which two obliquely propagating surface acoustic waves (SAWs) are launched \cite{SAW_grapheneMinguez_2018, AE_graphene_Mou_2025}. The interference of these SAWs generates a dynamically tunable superlattice potential within the 2D material, enabling in-situ control of topological phase transitions via the drive frequency and power.

The dynamical superlattice exhibits two key features. 
First, it introduces a new tunable length scale, ranging from tens of nanometers, (set by the highest accessible SAW frequencies in the GHz range), up to the electron scattering length, beyond which the potential is no longer coherently sampled \cite{SAW_45nm_Nelson_2012PRB, SAW_50G_Nelson, SAW_44G_2023}. This range bridges the gap between moiré and optical lattice periodicities and enables the formation of narrow, closely spaced energy bands (Fig.~\ref{fig: ASL}c).
Second, the frequency, amplitude, phase, and propagation direction of each SAW can be independently controlled. 
This enables the lattice geometry to be tuned independently of the potential amplitude, a capability unavailable in moiré systems, where the periodic potential is fixed by interlayer interactions.


Acoustoelectric superlattices offer several additional advantages over moiré superlattices. They eliminate the need for precise rotational alignment, thereby extending applicability to 2D materials that are not compatible with conventional tear-and-stack methods \cite{tearAndStack_Tutuc_2016, tearAndStack_Cao_2016}. Furthermore, ASLs avoid twist-angle disorder~\cite{Uri2020} and interlayer corrugation effects, which can induce uncontrolled and material-dependent modifications to the electronic structure \cite{corrugation_MATBG_2017_Nam, corrugation_MATBG_2019_Carr, corrugation_MATBG_2019_Lucignano}.

In the following we investigate the electronic structure of massive graphene subjected to an acoustoelectric superlattice, focusing on a single valley  (Fig.~\ref{fig: ASL}). The framework, however, is general and applies broadly to 2D materials.




\begin{figure*}[ht]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{images/fig1_merged.pdf}
    \caption{{ \bf Acoustoelectric superlattice: device, K-lattice, and band reconstruction.} 
  {(a)} Schematic of the device. A 2D material is placed on a piezoelectric substrate, separated by a spacer layer. 
  Two SAWs, propagating through the substrate, generate a superlattice potential in the 2D material. 
  {(b)} The SAW wavevectors $\bm{q}_1$ and $\bm{q}_2$ define a mBZ which tiles a K-lattice within the original BZ. 
  {(c)} The valence and conduction bands of the 2D material spilt into a set of minibands defined over the mBZ.}
    \label{fig: ASL}
\end{figure*}

\emph{Model ---}
The low-energy electronic structure of gapped graphene is described by the Hamiltonian
$ h_{\bm{k}} = v(\tau \sigma_x k_x + \sigma_y k_y) + m \sigma_z$,
where $\bm{k}$ denotes momentum  measured from the center of the $K$ or $K'$ valleys, $v$ is the Fermi velocity, $\sigma_i$ are the three Pauli matrices acting on the sublattice degree of freedom, and $\tau = \pm 1$ labels the valley index.  The mass term $m\sigma_z$ breaks sublattice symmetry and typically originates from a substrate such as hexagonal boron nitride~\cite{diracMass_Hunt_2013, diracMass_Woods_2014, diracMass_Jung_2015, diracMass_Liu_2022}.
The massive Dirac Hamiltonian carries a valley-dependent Chern number of $\pm 1/2$ in each band, reflecting its nontrivial topology. 



The SAW in the piezoelectric substrate couples to the 2D system via two distinct mechanisms. The first is the piezoelectric effect itself, where the electric field generated by the SAW directly interacts with the 2D electrons. The second is the deformation potential, arising from strain transferred from the vibrating substrate to the 2D layer. The latter effect is material-dependent; for instance, in graphene, time-dependent deformations generate space and time dependent pseudo-vector potentials that can significantly modify the band structure \cite{topologicalFlatbandsPerdiocStrain_KaiSun_2023, periodicStrain_Gao_Khalf_2023, flatBandsStrainedGraphene_Sandler_2023, moireByStrain_Escudero_2023}. In the following, we focus exclusively on the piezoelectric effect.
The deformation potential can be selectively suppressed by mechanically decoupling the 2D layer from the substrate, either by suspending it or by inserting a stiff spacer that blocks strain transmission (see Fig.~\ref{fig: ASL}a). Importantly, the piezoelectric field remains effective, as its evanescent decay into the spacer is negligible provided the spacer thickness is much smaller than the SAW wavelength and given that its dielectric constant is sufficiently small.

The resulting electric potential 
\be
V(\bm{r}) = g_1 \cos(\bm{q}_1 \cdot \bm{r} - \Omega_1 t) + g_2 \cos(\bm{q}_2 \cdot \bm{r} - \Omega_2 t)     \label{Vr}
\ee
oscillates in both space and time. It describes a moving superlattice characterized by the same wavevectors $\bm{q}_j$ and frequencies $\Omega_j= v_s q_j$ as the underlying SAWs, where $v_s$ is the substrate sound velocity for Rayleigh waves. The amplitude of each potential component is approximated by (cf. Supplementary Information~\footnote{In the Supplementary Information further details can be found regarding the transformation to the moving frame, about the derivation of the approximate ten-band description, as well as an extended discussion of the SAW potential and the Berry curvature of the minibands.}),
\be
g_j = \alpha_{\mathrm{PE}} \sqrt{P_j \lambda_j}, \qquad 
\alpha_{\mathrm{PE}} =  \sqrt{ \frac{K_{\mathrm{R}}^2 e^2 }{ \pi v_s \varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}} }
\label{gPE}
\ee
where $P_j$ is the SAW power per unit width and $\lambda_j = 2\pi/q_j$ is its wavelength. The coupling coefficient $\alpha_{\mathrm{PE}}$~\cite{SAW_Zhang} encodes the substrate parameters, including the electromechanical coupling coefficient
$K_{\mathrm{R}}^2$ and the effective surface dielectric constant $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}$. Screening by the 2D layer is neglected, which is justified for the gapped materials and the SAW wavelengths considered. 


As in moiré systems, it is useful to analyze the Hamiltonian in momentum space using the extended zone K-lattice framework
\cite{moireBands2011}. In this approach, crystal momentum 
$\bm{k}$ is confined to the mini Brillouin zone (mBZ) defined by the superlattice, while the full momentum space is partitioned into equivalence classes indexed by the set of vectors $\{  \bm{G}^{ij} \} = \{ i \bm{q}_1 +j \bm{q}_2 \}$, where $i$ and $j$ are integers, which span the reciprocal lattice of the superlattice. Each equivalence class, or K-lattice, includes momenta $\bm{G_k}^{ij} = \bm{k}+\bm{G}^{ij}$, and the periodic potential couples only states within the same K-lattice (see Fig.~\ref{fig: ASL}b). The effective Hamiltonian in a given K-lattice takes the form
\bea
H_{\bm{k}}(t) &=& \sum_{\bm{G}} \left\{ h_{\gsub{k}} c_{\gsub{k}}^\dagger c_{\gsub{k}} + \left[ g_1
e^{-i\Omega_1 t} c_{\gsub{k+q_1}}^\dagger c_{\gsub{k}} \right.  \right. \nonumber \\
&+& \left. \left.
g_2 e^{-i\Omega_2 t} c_{\gsub{k+q_2}}^\dagger c_{\gsub{k}} + \ \mathrm{h.c.}
\right] \right\}.   \label{Hk}
\eea
where h.c. denotes the Hermitian conjugate and internal degrees of freedom (e.g., spin, sublattice) are suppressed for brevity. 
In many cases, particularly when focusing on low-energy phenomena, the band structure $h_{\bm{k}}$ is approximated by a continuum model. As in moiré superlattices, the atomic-scale lattice becomes irrelevant, and the effective periodicity is dictated entirely by the superlattice potential.
