\emph{Moving frame ---}
The time-dependent Hamiltonian $H_{\bm{k}}(t)$ can be rendered time-independent via the unitary transformation $U_{\bm{k}}(t) = \exp{\lp i\Delta_{\bm{k}} t \rp}$ with
\be
\Delta_{\bm{k}} = \sum_{ij} \lp i \Omega_1 + j \Omega_2 \rp  c_{\gsub{k}^{ij}}^\dagger c_{\gsub{k}^{ij}}.    \label{Delta_k}
\ee
This transformation remains valid even when the drive frequencies $\Omega_1$ and $\Omega_2$ are incommensurate.
The wavefunction transforms accordingly as $|\psi'_{\bm{k}}(t)\ra = U_{\bm{k}}(t)|\psi_{\bm{k}}(t)\ra$, preserving the gauge invariance of physical observables.

The transformed Hamiltonian takes the form
\be
H'_{\bm{k}} = H_{\bm{k}}(0) - \Delta_{\bm{k}}  \label{Hk_movingFrame}
\ee
and acts in an extended Hilbert space indexed by the integers $(i,j)$, which  label replicas of the mBZ. The eigenvalues of $H'_{\bm{k}}$ define a set of quasi-energy bands. This construction generalizes the Floquet-Bloch formalism to accommodate cases where the driving is not strictly periodic.
When $\Omega_1$ and $\Omega_2$  are commensurate, $H_{\bm{k}}(t)$ is periodic in time, and the transformation reduces to the standard Floquet representation~\cite{Poertner2020,Wang2023a}. 
In the more general incommensurate case,  $H'_{\bm{k}}$ serves as an effective time-independent Hamiltonian whose spectrum functions as a generalized quasienergy band structure, facilitating the analysis of Berry curvature and topological response.



The Hamiltonian (\ref{Hk_movingFrame}) in the moving frame consists of two distinct contributions. The first one is a static component corresponding to the potential as seen in the moving frame. This term mimics a spatial modulation akin to that produced by a lithographically patterned substrate \cite{periodicStrain_Forsythe_2018, periodicStrain_Ruiz_2022}. The second term, $\Delta_{\bm{k}}$, varies linearly across the K-lattice and acts analogously to a uniform electric field in momentum space. It breaks the time-reversal, chiral, and inversion symmetries. 

Within each valley, $\Delta_{\bm{k}}$ yields a parametrically small energy shift. This follows from the effective truncation of the sum in Eq.~(\ref{Delta_k}) at order $g/v q$, making it small compared to $g$, the dominate energy scale in $H_{\bm{k}}(0)$.


However, $\Delta_{\bm{k}}$ becomes significant when comparing the energies of different valleys. In a static superlattice potential, the two valleys remain energetically degenerate. In contrast, in the moving frame, the moving superlattice introduces a directional bias, shifting the energy of one valley relative to the other by an amount
$2v_s k_D \approx 40$ meV where $k_D$ is the Dirac momentum. Despite its apparent magnitude, the physically meaningful valley-dependent shift is bounded by $\Omega \sim 4 \mu \mathrm{eV/GHz}$, which remains negligible even at the upper range of accessible SAW frequencies.
This is because inter-mBZ contributions are exactly canceled when transforming back to the laboratory frame via the inverse unitary transformation $U_{\bm{k}}^\dagger(t)$. 
Indeed, shifting $\Delta_{\bm{k}}$ by $\Delta_0 = \epsilon_0 \sum_{ij}  c_{\gsub{k}^{ij}}^\dagger c_{\gsub{k}^{ij}}$ with any constant $\epsilon_0$ leaves $|\psi_{\bm{k}}(t)\ra$ unchanged. This is akin to energy folding in Floquet theory where quasienergies are only defined modulo the drive frequency. 

\emph{Band reconstruction---}
The seemingly simple heterostructure in Fig.~\ref{fig: ASL}a supports a remarkably rich variety of band structures, governed by three tunable parameters: Dirac mass $m$, SAW wavelength $\lambda$, and SAW power $P$. Variation of these controls the mBZ size, minibands width, and the topological characteristics of the minibands.  

To gain insight into how ASLs reshape the band structure of 2D materials, we examine a symmetric configuration of two identical SAWs propagating along orthogonal directions, such that $q_1=q_2 \equiv q$ and $g_1=g_2 \equiv g$.

For numerical estimates, we consider a hBN spacer and a LiNbO$_3$ substrate, a common choice due to its strong piezoelectric response to Rayleigh waves. We adopt representative parameters of $v_s=3488$~m/s, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}=14 \varepsilon_0$, $\varepsilon_0$ being the vacuum permittivity, and $K_{\mathrm{R}}^2=0.05$. 
The band structure is obtained by diagonalizing $H'_{\bm{k}}$ throughout the mBZ. 
\begin{figure}[t]
  \centering
  \includegraphics[width=0.45\textwidth]{images/chern_map_final_fix.jpg}
  \vspace{0.5cm}
  \hspace{-0.7cm} 
  \includegraphics[width=0.41\textwidth]{images/plot_dirictgap_23.jpg}
  \caption{{\bf Valley Chern number.} (a) Support of $C_v$ in the $m$-$\lambda$ plane for $P=5$~W/m. (b) Direct and indirect gaps between valence and conduction minibands along the dotted line in (a). Topological valley-Hall response occurs for $37~\textrm{nm} \lesssim \lambda \lesssim 40~\textrm{nm}$ when the indirect gap is positive. }
  \label{fig: Cv}
\end{figure}


SAW can induce band inversions. This is illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig: band evolution} where the band structure is plotted along high symmetry lines in the mBZ for $m=20$~meV and $P=1$~W/m in the range of $55 ~\textrm{nm} \leq \lambda \leq 68 ~\textrm{nm}$, close to charge neutrality ($E=0$). At short wavelengths, the mBZ is relatively large and the massive Dirac cone is only mildly perturbed. As the SAW wavelength $\lambda$ increases, the coupling strength grows, triggering sequential band inversions and topological phase transitions. A first gap closing between the valence and conduction minibands occurs at $\Gamma$  near $\lambda \approx 63$~nm, after which the highest valence band acquires a valley Chern number $C_v=-1$. A second gap closing at the $M$-point near $\lambda \approx 66$~nm, returns the valence band to a topologically trivial phase with $C_v=0$. 

A more comprehensive map of $C_v$ of the highest valence miniband is given in Fig.~\ref{fig: Cv}a in which the valley Chern number is plotted as a function of $\lambda$ and $m$ for $P=5$~W/m. Non-zero $C_v$ values  are supported over a wide continuous range of parameters in the $m$-$\lambda$ plane. At short wavelengths $C_v=0$. Increasing $\lambda$, the valley Chern number generically attains the value $-1$ at a mass-dependent wavelength because the conduction and valence band cross. As $\lambda$ is further increased, $C_v$ is changed multiple times at different wavelength, often because of crossings of two valence bands. While we focus on the highest valence miniband, gap closings are not unique to bands near charge neutrality but also occur for more remote energy minibands.

A topological valley-Hall response is possible provided that the chemical potential can be placed in a gap above the non-trivial miniband. The existence of fully gapped states can be confirmed by plotting the indirect gap for the top valence band. For example, Fig.~\ref{fig: Cv}b implies a  topological valley-Hall response for $37~\textrm{nm} \lesssim \lambda \lesssim 40~\textrm{nm}$ where the indirect gap is positive. 

The oscillatory behavior of the energy gap $E_g$ as a function of $\lambda$, depicted in Fig.~\ref{fig: Cv}b, appears for all values of $m$ and $P$.  
Gap closings often occur near the $\Gamma$ and $M$ points but are not restricted to high symmetry points.
The value of $E_g$ for small $\lambda$ up to the first gap closing is approximately captured by a minimal ten-band model, which retains only the four nearest-neighbors on the K-lattice for each momentum in the mBZ. As shown in the Supplementary Information~\cite{Note1}, this model predicts the first gap closing at $g_c=\sqrt{\lp v q \rp^2 + m^2-\Omega^2}$. 



As the wavelength of the SAW increases, the mBZ shrinks and the minibands become increasingly dense. One might expect a trivial folding of the massive-graphene spectrum at large wavelengths, where the superlattice potential becomes nearly uniform on atomic scales. On the contrary, as illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig: dense bands}, flat well-separated minibands emerge for specific points in the $\lambda$-$P$-$m$ parameter space. This counterintuitive result stems from the wavelength dependence of the piezoelectric coupling $g_{\mathrm{PE}}$. Unlike the deformation potential, which scales as $1/\sqrt{\lambda}$, the piezoelectric coupling increases with $\lambda$, simultaneously shrinking the mBZ and enhancing coupling.
\begin{figure}[htp]
    \includegraphics[width=1\columnwidth, height=6cm]{images/atomlike_final_fixed_2.jpg}
    \caption{{\bf Long wavelength minibands.} (a) Atomic-like spectra for $\lambda = 180$~nm, $m=40$~meV, and $P = 0.1 $~W/m. (b) Minibands away from neutrality exhibiting non-zero valley Chern numbers for $\lambda=158$~nm, $m=20$~meV and $P=0.2$~W/m.}
    \label{fig: dense bands}
\end{figure}

The large $\lambda$-$m$-$P$ parameter space gives rise to very different types of band structures. For large mass value the energy dispersion near the Dirac point flattens. The folding on the spectra to the mBZ can then yield a sequence of highly atomic-like flat bands. For the parameters used in Fig.~\ref{fig: dense bands}a the bandwidth of the minibands near charge neutrality is below $1$~meV. Non-zero values of $C_v$ emerge also away from the neutrality point as illustrated in  Fig.~\ref{fig: dense bands}b, however these typically appear in more dispersive minibands.



\emph{Berry curvature---}
For the continuum band structure, in the absence of SAWs, the valence and conduction bands carry valley Chern numbers of $C_v = \pm 1/2$ per spin. 
In contrast, the presence of a superlattice enforces integer-valued $C_v$.
This strong modification of the quantum geometry is accomplished by an additional Berry curvature contribution which emerges at the mBZ boundary and cancels the Berry curvature near the Dirac point, resulting in the vanishing of $C_v$~\cite{Note1}. Similar compensatory behavior has been reported for CDW-modulated continuum models~\cite{Tan2024}.
Interestingly, we observe that the Berry curvature is strongly localized at the BZ center and at the corners, even away from the band crossing and when the band gaps are well developed. As can be calculated from Fig.~\ref{fig: band evolution}e, the valley Chern number receives almost 60\% of its weight from only 10\% of the mBZ area~\cite{Note1}.
This behavior of the Berry curvature is not unlike in magic-angle twisted bilayer graphene (MATBG), where a sharply peaked distribution of the Berry curvature is thought to play an important factor in the cascade of correlated phases which emerge upon band flattening~\cite{Ledwith2025,Ledwith2025a}. 

Finally, we point out that because $\Delta_{\bm{k}}$ only weakly breaks time-reversal, chiral and inversion symmetries, opposite valleys retain opposite valley Chern numbers, preserving their relative topological structure.


\emph{Discussion.---}
We introduced a new class of periodic structures, the \emph{acoustoelectric superlattices}, analyzed their distinctive properties, and showed that their band structures can be widely tuned using accessible experimental knobs. 
For the highest SAW power used in our analysis (5~W/m), and for a device width of approximately $5 \mu m$ the total power is about $25$~mW, well within the cooling capacity of standard cryogenic setups. 
While generating a surface acoustic wave with short wavelength poses nontrivial experimental challenges, substantial progress has been made in recent years \cite{SAW_45nm_Nelson_2012PRB, SAW_50G_Nelson, SAW_44G_2023}, bringing $\lambda \gtrsim 70$~nm within current experimental reach. Achieving shorter wavelength will require experimental innovation. 

Although SAWs weakly break time-reversal symmetry,  valley degeneracy is preserved to an excellent approximation in the absence of additional perturbations. This degeneracy can, however, be deliberately lifted by introducing explicit time-reversal-breaking mechanisms, for example via proximity to a magnetic substrate~\cite{valleyDeg_Wei_2016, valleyDeg_Zhao_2017, valleyDeg_Norden_2019}. 

We anticipate that the flat bands induced by ASLs will host strongly correlated electronic states.
The Coulomb energy scale can be estimated as
$ E_c \approx  \frac{\alpha_g}{\epsilon_r} \frac{v}{\lambda} $ where $\alpha_g = e^2/(4\pi \epsilon_0 \hbar v) \approx 2.2$ is the fine-structure constant of graphene~\cite{CastroNeto2009}. This scale decreases with increasing wavelength and evaluates to approximately $8\mathrm{meV}/\epsilon_r$  at $\lambda=180$~nm. The bandwidth  of the flat bands in Fig.~\ref{fig: dense bands}a are below $W\approx1$meV, yielding a ratio $E_c/W \approx 2$ for $\epsilon_r = 4$. This is comparable to estimates of the e-e interaction in MATBG, where flat bands have been shown to amplify interaction effects \cite{MATBG_ee_Bultinck_2020, MATBG_ee_Xie_2020, MATBG_ee_Ledwith_2021, MATBG_ee_Song_2022, MATBG_ee_Xiao_2025}. A key distinction, however, is that in MATBG the superlattice potential must be solved self-consistently with the band structure, whereas in ASLs it is externally imposed by the SAWs.

A unique advantage of ASLs is their ability to support in-situ band structure tuning, enabling real-time control of electronic properties. Given the widespread use of surface-acoustic waves in modern technologies, it will be intriguing to explore whether ASLs, with intentionally broken valley symmetry, can function as topological switches.

Our analysis has focused on a simplified configuration in which the superlattice is generated by two orthogonal SAWs with identical wavelength and power. However, much richer superlattice geometries are possible. By varying the number of SAWS and tuning their power, wavelength, phase, and propagation direction, a large family of complex potential landscapes can be designed. 
This flexibility opens the door to engineering band structures tailored to support targeted electronic and topological phases, including highly correlated states.

Other 2D materials could likewise serve as electronic platforms for ASLs, offering additional avenues for band-structure control. For example, the spectrum of multilayer graphene can be tuned by applying a displacement field, while transition metal dichalcogenides exhibit spin–valley locking. An advantage of graphene-based systems is their exceptionally long mean free path, which makes them especially well suited for superlattices with relatively large periodicities.
%
Finally, we expect that ASLs will give rise to interesting topological  transport responses. Exploring these effects lies beyond the scope of the present work. 

