\section{Moving frame}

We look for a unitary transformation that makes the Hamiltonian $H_{\bm{k}}(t)$ in Eq.~\eqref{Hk} of the main text time-independent. With a general unitary operator $U_{\bm{k}}$ the Hamiltonian transforms as 
\be
H'_{\bm{k}} = U_{\bm{k}} H_{\bm{k}} U_{\bm{k}}^\dagger +i \frac{\partial U_{\bm{k}}}{\partial t} U_{\bm{k}}^\dagger          \label{Hkp}
\ee
and the wavefunction is changed accordingly $|\psi'_{\bm{k}}(t)\ra = U_{\bm{k}}(t)|\psi_{\bm{k}}(t)\ra$ to satisfy the time-dependent Schrodinger equation.
We look for an operator of the form 
\be
U_{\bm{k}}(t) = e^{i F_{\bm{k}}(t)},
\ee
where
\be
F_{\bm{k}} = \sum_{\bm{G}} \alpha_{\gsub{k}}(t) c_{\gsub{k}}^\dagger c_{\gsub{k}},      \label{Fk}
\ee
with $\bm{G}$ being the reciprocal lattice vectors of the superlattice and find $\alpha_{\gsub{k}}(t)$ by requiring $H'_{\bm{k}}$ to be time-independent. The transformation of the Hamiltonian under $U_{\bm{k}}(t)$ follows from the transformation of the annihilation operator $c_{\bm{k}}$ under this operator. Using the Baker-Campbell-Hausdorff relation:
\be
U_{\bm{k}} c_{\bm{\scriptscriptstyle G_{k}}} U_{\bm{k}}^\dagger = c_{\bm{\scriptscriptstyle G_{k}}} + [iF_{\bm{k}}, c_{\bm{\scriptscriptstyle G_{k}}}] + \frac{1}{2!} [iF_{\bm{k}}, [iF_{\bm{k}}, c_{\bm{\scriptscriptstyle G_{k}}}]] + \ldots.
\ee
and the commutator,
\be
[iF_{\bm{k}}, c_{\bm{\scriptscriptstyle G_{k}}}] =  i\sum_{\bm{G}} \alpha_{\gsub{k}}(t) [c_{\gsub{k}}^\dagger c_{\gsub{k}}, c_{\bm{\scriptscriptstyle G_{k}}}] = -i \alpha_{\bm{\scriptscriptstyle G_{k}}}(t) c_{\bm{\scriptscriptstyle G_{k}}}
\ee
we find 
\be
c_{\gsub{k}} \to c_{\gsub{k}} \exp{\left[-i \alpha_{\gsub{k}}(t)\right]}
\label{c_Utransform}
\ee
The transformation of the creation operator can be obtained by taking the hermitian conjugate of Eq.(\ref{c_Utransform}).
It follows that, under $U_{\bm{k}}(t)$, the $g_1$ hopping term in $H_{\bm{k}}(t)$ acquires the phase 
\be
\phi_1(t) = -\Omega_1 t - \alpha_{\gsub{k}}(t) + \alpha_{\gsub{k+q_1}}(t).      \label{phi1}
\ee
A similar phase is obtained for the $\bm{q}_2$ hopping term.
The phases vanish, making the Hamiltonian $H_{\bm{k}}(t)$ time-independent, provided that 
\be
\alpha_{\gsub{k+q_j}} - \alpha_{\gsub{k}}(t)  = \Omega_j t  \label{alpha_eqs}
\ee
for j=1,2. Solving Eq.~\eqref{alpha_eqs}, we find  
\be
\alpha_{\gsub{k}^{ij}}(t) = \lp i \Omega_1 + j \Omega_2 \rp t
\label{alpha_sol}
\ee
independent of $\bm{k}$. The reference position from which the $i,j$ indices in Eq.~\eqref{alpha_sol} are enumerated can be chosen arbitrarily. However, shifting the origin of the K-lattice and has no physical consequence. Substituting Eq.~\eqref{alpha_sol}
in Eq.~\eqref{Fk} yields 
$ U_{\bm{k}} = \exp{\lp i\Delta_{\bm{k}} t \rp} $
where $\Delta_{\bm{k}}$ is given by Eq.~\ref{Delta_k}.

\section{10-band model}
In the limit of weak SAW coupling $g_j/vq \ll 1$, the problem simplifies because the amplitude of the wavefunction is concentrated on the $\kk$-state in the mBZ and its four nearest neighbors on the K-lattice, coupled via the SAW wavevectors $\pm \bm{q}_1$ and $\pm \bm{q}_2$ (see  Fig.~\ref{fig: ten_band_schem}a). The wavefunction amplitude on more distant K-lattice sites is negligible. Including both graphene sublattices at each momentum thus yields a Hamiltonian with 10 bands. 

\begin{figure*}[htp]
    \centering
    \begin{minipage}[b]{0.5\linewidth}
        \centering
        \raisebox{0.5cm}{\includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{images/10_band_modle_fig.jpg}}
    \end{minipage}
    \hspace{-1.5cm} 
    \begin{minipage}[b]{0.5\linewidth}
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.9\linewidth]{images/gap_gamma_final_10.png}
    \end{minipage}
    \caption{{\bf 10 band model.} (a) The truncated K-lattice of the 10-band model includes 5 k-sites. (b) A comparison between $E_g(\lambda)$, the energy gap between the valence and conduction minibands at the Dirac point, calculated for $P = 1$~W/m and $m = 10$~meV using the full K-lattice and the 10-band model.}
    \label{fig: ten_band_schem}
\end{figure*}



In this weak coupling regime, the energy gap $E_g$ between the conduction and valence minibands first closes at the Dirac point.  One can therefore use the 10-band model to analytically find $g_c$, the smallest value of $g$ for which $E_g$ vanishes. We numerically check the accuracy of our result by comparing the value of $E_g$ obtained from the 10-band model with a computation that uses the full K-lattice.  

As elucidated in the main text, we consider two orthogonal SAWs with the same amplitude $g$ and same wavevector magnitude $\bm{q}_1 = q \bm{\hat{x}}$ and $\bm{q}_2 = q \bm{\hat{y}}$. The time-dependence of the Hamiltonian is removed by changing to the moving frame where
\begin{align}\label{full_ham_of}
H'_{\bm{k}} = \sum_{ij} \Bigl[ h\bigl(\kk_{ij}\bigr) - \bigl(i \Omega + j \Omega \bigr) \Bigr] c_{ij}^\dagger c_{ij} + \frac{g}{2} \sum_{ij} \Bigl[c_{i+1,j}^\dagger c_{ij} + c_{i,j+1}^\dagger c_{ij} + \text{h.c.} \Bigr]\,
\end{align}
Here $c_{ij}$ annihilates an electron from a state $\kk_{ij} = \kk+n_i \bm{q}_i + n_j \bm{q}_j$ on the K-lattice, $\kk$ is a lattice momentum inside the mBZ, and $\Omega=v_s q$  where $v_s$ is the speed of sound in the substrate. The massive Dirac Hamiltonian in the sublattice basis, is
$ h_\kk=m\sigma_z+v\,(\tau\sigma_xk_x+\sigma_yk_y)$,
where $\sigma_i$ are the Pauli matrices, $\tau=\pm 1$ labels the K and K' valleys, and $v$ is the Dirac velocity.

Within the framework of the 10-band model, the Hamiltonian in Eq.~\eqref{full_ham_of} is truncated to a $10\times10$ matrix which assumes the form
\begin{equation} \label{eig_eq} \cH_0=\begin{pmatrix}
        h_0&t&t&t&t\\
        t&h_{r} - \Omega &0&0&0\\
        t&0&h_{l} + \Omega&0&0\\
        t&0&0&h_{b} + \Omega &0\\
        t&0&0&0&h_{t} - \Omega
    \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
at the Dirac point.
Here the matrices on the diagonal are given by the massive Dirac Hamiltonian at momenta $0, \pm\bm{q}_1, \pm\bm{q}_2$ and $t=t^\dagger = g/2 \cdot \sigma_0$ is the hopping matrix. The Hamiltonian acts on five two-component spinors $\Psi=(\phi_0, \phi_1, \phi_2, \phi_3, \phi_4)$. 
The zero energy solution, $\cH_0 \Psi = 0$ leads to five matrix equations.
The first equation 
\begin{eqnarray}
    t \phi_0&+&(h_r-\Omega)\phi_1=0
    \nonumber\\
    \Rightarrow\phi_1&=&\frac{t}{\Omega^2-m^2-v^2q^2}\lt(\Omega\sigma_0+\tau vq\sigma_x+m\sigma_z\rt)\phi_0
\end{eqnarray}
relates $\phi_1$ to $\phi_0$. Similar equations express $\phi_2,\phi_3,\textrm{and}\,\phi_4$ in terms of $\phi_0$.
Eliminating the other $\phi_i$ in favor of $\phi_0$ yields the condition,
\begin{align}
    0&=h_0\phi_0+t_c(\phi_1+\phi_2+\phi_3+\phi_4)\\
    \Leftrightarrow\qquad 0&=\lp 1-\frac{g_c^2}{v^2q^2+m^2-\Omega^2} \rp\sigma_z\phi_0=0
\end{align}
which is satisfied by choosing
$g_c=\sqrt{v^2q^2+m^2-\Omega^2}$.
Fig.~\ref{fig: ten_band_schem}b compares between the numerical solutions for $E_g$ obtained using the full K-lattice and the 10-band model. Good agreement is observed up to $\lambda \approx 30$~nm. For larger wavelengths (corresponding to stronger effective interactions), more K-lattice sites must be included. 


