\section{SAW-induced potential}


We derive the scalar potential $V(x,t)$ induced in a two-dimensional material by a single Rayleigh-type SAW propagating along the x-direction on the surface of a piezoelectric substrate located at $z=0$. The SAW induces both an electric field and mechanical strain, leading to two additive contributions:
\be
V(x,t) = g \cos(qx - \Omega t), \quad g = g_{\mathrm{PE}} + g_{\mathrm{DP}}.
\ee
Below we compute the piezoelectric part $g_{\mathrm{PE}}$ in terms of substrate parameters. As explained in the main text, the system we consider is intentionally designed to suppress the deformation potential contribution $g_{\mathrm{DP}}$.

The SAW generates an electric potential $\phi(x,z,t)$ that extends into both the substrate and the surrounding medium. Near the surface, we approximate it as 
\be
\phi(x,z,t) = \phi_0 e^{i(qx - \Omega t)} e^{-q b(z) |z|}.
\ee
A full solution of the piezoelectric equations couples the displacements and the electric potential \cite{SAW_Zhang} and defines the electromechanical coupling coefficient $U_E/U=K_R^2$, where $U$ is  total SAW energy per unit area  and $U_E$ the fraction of this energy which is stored in the electric field. 
Here we employ a different approach in which $K_R^2$ is taken from experiment, and $\phi$ is approximated by a single mode with a decay coefficient $b(z)$ which takes the value $b(z)=1$ outside the substrate and the value $b(z)=b_s$ inside. The amplitude $\phi_0$ is chosen so that the electric‐field energy matches the known piezoelectric fraction $K_R^2$. 

The total SAW energy per unit area is
\be
U = \frac{P}{v_s}\,,
\ee
where $P$ is the acoustic power per unit width and $v_s$ the SAW velocity.  A fraction $K_R^2$ of this energy is stored in the electric field,
\be
U_E = K_R^2\,\frac{P}{v_s}\,.
\ee
On the other hand, using $\bm E=-\nabla\phi$ and integrating the field energy density over z gives
\be
U_E = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\!\!dz\;\frac12\,\varepsilon(z)\,\lvert \bm E(z)\rvert^2 =
\frac12\,\varepsilon_{\rm eff}\,q\,\lvert\phi_0\rvert^2,
\ee
with the effective permittivity $\varepsilon_{\rm eff} = \frac{1}{2}(\varepsilon_{\rm env}+b_s\,\varepsilon_{\rm HF}) $. We neglect screening by the 2D layer because our focus is on regimes where the chemical potential lies in energy gaps or on highly correlated flat bands that screen poorly.
\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=7cm]{images/g_PE_vs_lambda.png}
    \caption{{\bf Piezoelectric coupling.} The coupling constant $g_{\mathrm{PE}}$ as a function of wavelength for $P = 1$W/m, a LiNbO$_3$ substrate and a hBN spacer.}
    \label{fig: inducedPotential}
\end{figure}

Equating the two expressions for $U_E$ and using $g_{\mathrm{PE}} = e|\phi_0|$ yields 
\be
g_{\mathrm{PE}} = \alpha_{\mathrm{PE}} \sqrt{P \lambda} \label{g_PE}, \ \ \ 
\alpha_{\mathrm{PE}} =  \sqrt{ \frac{K_R^2 e^2 }{ \pi v_s \varepsilon_{\mathrm{eff}}} } 
\ee
where $\lambda = 2\pi/q$ is the SAW wavelength.


The value of $\alpha_{\mathrm{PE}}$ depends on the choice of materials, stacking order, crystal cut, and device dimensions and can therefore vary substantially (a simple schematic is shown in Fig.~\ref{fig: ASL}a in the main text). 
A good design will maximize the piezoelectric coupling while suppressing strain transfer from the substrate. Here we provide an estimate assuming a LiNbO$_3$ substrate and an hBN spacer.   
For LiNbO$_3$ we use $v_s = 3488 \ \mathrm{m/s}, \quad \varepsilon_{\mathrm{HF}} = 40\varepsilon_0, \quad K_R^2 = 0.05$, and $b_s=0.6$,  with $\varepsilon_{\rm env}=4\varepsilon_0$. Measuring $g$ in meV, $P$ in W/m and $\lambda$ in nm, gives $ \alpha_{\mathrm{PE}} =  6.1 $. 
The coupling $g_{\mathrm{PE}}$ is plotted in Fig.~\ref{fig: inducedPotential}. 


\section{Berry curvature for weak SAWs}

The Chern number, defined for a periodic two-dimensional system, is a topological invariant that must take integer values. In graphene, the Chern numbers of the valence and conduction bands vanish. However, the Berry curvature is sharply peaked near the Dirac points, giving rise to an approximate value of $\pm 1/2$ for the enclosed Berry curvature per spin in each valley near $K$ and $K'$, respectively. 
In the continuum model, where the underlying lattice is absent, this leads to a formal assignment of Chern number $\pm 1/2$ to each valley, per spin. 

An intriguing situation arises when weak SAWs induce a 2D periodic potential on the continuum model. The superlattice periodicity enforces the valley Chern number to become an integer. To examine how the Berry curvature is redistributed to satisfy this constraint, 
Fig.~\ref{fig: BC distribution} shows its distribution over the first mBZ across two band inversions for a narrow range of wavelengths near to $\lambda\approx 66$nm (cf.~Fig.~\ref{fig: BC distribution}a).

\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=10cm]{images/5_Berry_Curvature_final.jpg}
    \caption{
    {\bf Berry curvature distribution.} 
    Distribution of the Berry curvature over the mBZ for the top valence miniband at $P = 1$~W/m and $m=20$~meV, for different wavelengths. (a) Two band inversions at $\Gamma$ and $M$ take place as the wavelength increases from $\lambda \approx 58$~nm to $\lambda \approx 70$~nm. (b-f)  The evolution of the Berry curvature distribution across the two band inversions.
    }
    \label{fig: BC distribution}
\end{figure}

Prior to the first band inversion (Fig.~\ref{fig: BC distribution}b), there is a large Berry curvature near the Dirac point, which is characteristic of the continuum model. This Berry curvature is balanced by a negative contribution near the edges of the mBZ, resulting in a net valley Chern number $C_v=0$.
As the SAW wavelength increases, a topological phase transition occurs, leading to a valley Chern number of one (Fig.~\ref{fig: BC distribution}c. With further increase in $\lambda$, a second band inversion occurs near the $M$-point (Fig.~\ref{fig: BC distribution}e,f) reverting $C_v$ back to zero.
The Berry-curvature maps in Fig.~\ref{fig: BC distribution} were calculated for $m=20$~meV and $P = 1$~W/m.


Interestingly, even at $\lambda \approx 66$~nm when the energy gap between the valence and conduction minibands reaches its maximum, the Berry curvature remains peaked around the $\Gamma$ and $M$ points. The cumulative Berry curvature near these points is plotted in Fig.~\ref{fig: cumulative BC}. Even when the minibands are well separated, approximately 10\% of the mBZ accounts for nearly 60\% of the valley Chern number.




\begin{figure}[h!]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=6cm]{images/chern_vs_area.png}
    \caption{{\bf Highly localized Berry curvature.}
    The cumulative fraction of the total Berry curvature as a function of the cumulative BZ area fraction, with k-points ordered from highest to lowest Berry curvature density. The curve is calculated at $\lambda \approx 66$nm corresponding to Fig.~\ref{fig: BC distribution}d.}
    \label{fig: cumulative BC}
\end{figure}

