\section{Fluid Aerial Network: Maximizing the System Sum Rate by UAV Rotation}
%In this section, we apply UAV rotation to the UAV-enabled cellular communication system to reduce inter-cell interference and improve the system sum rate. First, we formulate a rate maximization problem with UAV orientations as variables, and then present a low-complexity  algorithm to determine the optimal rotation angles for each UAV.

In this section, we employ the UAV rotation to a UAV-enabled multi-cell and multi-user cellular communication system with the objective of mitigating inter-cell interference and enhancing the overall system sum rate. In this direction, we first formulate a sum-rate maximization problem where the UAV rotation angles serve as optimization variables. Then, a low-complexity algorithm is proposed to determine the optimal orientations for each UAV.  

%\textcolor{red}{[DELETE]Our optimization method is based on location information. First, the ABS needs to know the \textcolor{black}{locations} of the GUs in its serving cell, which can be achieved by receiving global positioning system location reports from the GUs or by estimating the angle of arrival using uplink reference signals. Furthermore, the UAVs need to know the location information of the GUs in other cells, which can be obtained by information exchange between adjacent UAVs. This scheme is primarily intended for scenarios in which the GU is stationary or moving slowly because moving GUs at high speeds can hinder information exchange between UAVs, and UAVs cannot adjust their angles promptly.}

