\section{Introduction}
The \emph{knot Floer order} $\Ord(K)$ of a knot $K$ in $S^3$ was introduced in~\cite{JuhMilZem2020cobordism} as the torsion order of the $\F[U]$-module $\HFK^-(K)$. It offers new lower bounds for knot invariants that were previously difficult to estimate, such as the \emph{fusion number} for ribbon knots (see e.g.~\cite{KaMa1997Ribbon, HoKaPa2021Ribbon}), \emph{band unlinking number}(see e.g.~\cite{Lic1986bandunlinking, HoYa1990bandunlinking}), and \emph{bridge index} (see e.g.~\cite{Schubert1954Bruecken, schultens2001additivitybridgenumberknots}), which is of particular relevance to our work. Juhász, Miller, and Zemke~\cite{JuhMilZem2020cobordism} established that 
\begin{equation}
\label{ineq: ord and br}
    \Ord(K) + 1 \leq \br(K).
\end{equation}
We prove that the lower bound in Equation~\eqref{ineq: ord and br} is multiplicative under cabling for L-space cables of L-space knots. For $p$ and $q$ coprime, the $(p,q)$-cable of a knot $K$, denoted $K_{p,q}$, refers to the satellite knot with companion $K$ and pattern $T_{p,q}$. 

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative}
    Let \(K_{p,q}\) be an L-space knot in \(S^3 \) that is the $(p,q)$-cable of an L-space knot \(K\) in \(S^3\) with Seifert genus $g(K) > 1$. Then 
    \[
    \Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 = p \cdot (\Ord(K) + 1).
    \]
\end{theorem}

A knot is called an \emph{L-space knot} if it admits a positive Dehn surgery to an \emph{L-space} -- a 3-manifold with the "simplest" Heegaard Floer homology. They were introduced in~\cite{OS2005lensespacesurgeries} and have notable properties, e.g., being fibered~\cite{Ni2007fibered}. Among the L-space knots, the trefoil knot \(T_{2,3}\) stands out as the unique genus-one knot\footnote{It is one of two fibered genus-one knots~\cite[5.14 Proposition]{BurZieHeu2014knots}. However, the Figure-8 knot is not an L-space knot, which can be checked using e.g.~\cite{Szabo_calculator}. }. Notably, an analogue of Theorem~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative} also holds for \(T_{2,3}\).

\begin{theorem}
    \label{thm: mult for T23}
    Let \(K\) denote the trefoil knot \(T_{2,3}\), and let \(K_{p,q}\) be its \((p,q)\)-cable, where \(p\) and \(q\) are coprime integers. 
    \begin{align*}
        \Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 = \left\{
        \begin{array}{cc}
            q & p < q < 2p \\
            2p & 2p < q
        \end{array}
        \right.
    \end{align*}
\end{theorem}

Our results have particular relevance to the calculation of the bridge index and thus to the \emph{BB-Conjecture}~\cite{KriMor2025bbconjecture}. It states that the bridge index $\br(K)$ and the \emph{braid index} $\operatorname{b}(K)$ agree in the case of L-space knots. Both knot invariants are known to be multiplicative under cabling~\cite{williams1992multiplicative}, and $\br(K) \leq \operatorname{b}(K)$, but both are generally difficult to calculate.

Assuming $p \cdot (\Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1) > \Ord(K) + 1$ yields that $p\cdot\br(K) = \br(K_{p,q}) \geq \Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 > p \cdot ( \Ord(K) + 1)$ thus giving a higher bound on $\br(K)$ then Equation~\eqref{ineq: ord and br}. We prove in Theorem~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative} that this is not the case. 

Even more, Juh\'asz, Miller, and Zemke prove in~\cite[Corollary~5.3]{JuhMilZem2020cobordism} that 
\begin{align}
\label{eq: Ord detects br}
\br(T_{p,q}) = \Ord(T_{p,q}) + 1
\end{align}
for torus knots $T_{p,q}$. Krishna and Morton~\cite[Equation~3.2, p.7]{KriMor2025bbconjecture} prove Equation~\eqref{eq: Ord detects br} for L-space knots which are braid closures of braids whose braid word includes a full twist, and our data in~\cite{census_knot_paper} provides only 9 possible counterexamples to Equation~\eqref{eq: Ord detects br}. Thus we ask

\begin{question}
\label{qu: Ord+1 = br}
    Let $K$ be an L-space knot in $S^3$. Is $\Ord(K) + 1$ equal to the bridge index $\br(K)$? 
\end{question}

In the case of L-space knots, Juh\'asz, Miller, and Zemke~\cite[Lemma~5.1]{JuhMilZem2020cobordism} prove that the Alexander polynomial $\Delta_K(t)$ detects $\Ord(K)$ as the highest difference of neighboring exponents.
\begin{align}
    \label{eq: Ordv from alex}
    \Ord(K) = \max\{ \alpha_{i-1} - \alpha_i \mid i = 0,\dots, 2n\},
\end{align}
where $\alpha_i$ are the exponents in $\Delta_K(t)$ in decreasing order. We tried proving Theorem~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative} using the known cabling formula for the Alexander polynomial (see e.g.~\cite[Theorem~6.15]{Lickorish1997introduction})
\[
\Delta_{K_{p,q}}(t) = \Delta_K(t^p) \cdot \Delta_{T_{p,q}}(t).
\]
It proved to be very difficult, and it is due to the ease of a new tool -- immersed curves, introduced in~\cite{HanRasWat2022heegaardfortorusbdd, HanRasWat2024borderedviaimmersed} -- that we did not pursue a proof using the Alexander polynomial. The immersed curve invariant $\widehat{\HF}(M)$ consists of curves in a punctured torus and encodes a certain bordered invariant. In the case of knot exteriors $M = S^3 \setminus \operatorname{int}(\nu K)$, it decodes $\HFK^-(K)$ and thus $\Ord(K)$. It behaves nicely under cabling~\cite{HanWat2023cabling} and for L-space knots~\cite[Section 7.5]{HanRasWat2024borderedviaimmersed}. The proof is an explicit geometric argument using the immersed curves. However, we want to encourage any reader to prove Theorem~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative} using $\Delta_K(t)$ or possibly extend the structure results on the Alexander polynomial of L-space knots (see e.g.~\cite{Krcatovich2018restriction}).

We collected counterexamples to Theorem~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative} in the case where $K$ is a knot in $S^3$ that is not an L-space knot in~\cite{GitHub}. During this search, a new pattern emerged from the data, resembling the behavior of cables whose companion knots satisfy the BB-conjecture.
 Krishna and Morton~\cite{KriMor2025bbconjecture} proved that if the BB-conjecture holds for an L-space knot, it also holds for all of its L-space cables. Our data suggests that the multiplicity condition exhibits a similar pattern.

\begin{question}
\label{q: mult for cables}
Let $K$ be an arbitrary knot in $S^3$. Suppose $q > p\cdot(2g(K) - 1)$ if $g(K) > 1$, or $q > 2p$ if $g(K) = 1$, and suppose there exists such a $(p,q)$-cable $K_{p,q}$ of $K$ satisfying
\[
\Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 = p \cdot (\Ord(K) + 1).
\]
Does this equality hold for all such cables of $K$?
\end{question}

The bounds in Question~\ref{q: mult for cables} are not arbitrary. The behavior of cables of L-space knots has been studied by Hedden~\cite{Hedden2009cabling} and Hom~\cite{Hom2011cabling}, who classified all slopes $p/q \in \Q$ for which the $(p,q)$-cable of an L-space knot is again an L-space knot. This occurs if and only if \(q > p \cdot (2g(K) + 1)\), a result later reproven using immersed curves in~\cite{HanWat2023cabling}. They ensure that in the construction of the immersed curve for a cable~\cite[Theorem 1]{HanWat2023cabling}, two copies do not overlap in height (cf. Section~\ref{sec: proof of theorem}).

This paper is a follow-up to~\cite{census_knot_paper}, which is not yet published. Theorem~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative} was conjectured based on the data obtained by studying the bridge index of the census knots in an ongoing project~\cite{census_knot_paper}. The explicit computations that led to the main theorem and both questions are publicly available in~\cite{GitHub}. Both computational projects use the data from~\cite{knotinfo}. We will introduce the prerequisites in Section~\ref{sec: prereq}, including knot Floer homology, L-space knots, and their immersed curve invariant $\widehat{\HF}(K)$. The main results to obtain $\widehat{\HF}(K)$ from $\HFK^-(K)$ for arbitrary knots can be found in~\cite{HanRasWat2022heegaardfortorusbdd}. Section~\ref{sec: proof of theorem} includes the proofs of both Theorems. 

