\section{Proof of Theorems}
\label{sec: proof of theorem}

We start by proving Lemma~\ref{lem: pegs to Ord}, which will be essential in the proof of both theorems. It proves how $\Ord(K)$ can be read from the immersed curves for L-space knots.

\begin{lemma}
\label{lem: pegs to Ord}
    Let \( K \) in \(S^3 \) be an L-space knot, and let \( \widetilde{\gamma} \subset [-1/2, 1/2] \times \R \) denote the lift of the immersed curve invariant \( \widehat{\HF}(M) \) for the knot complement \( M = S^3 \setminus \operatorname{int}(\nu K) \). Then the knot Floer order \( \Ord(K) \) is equal to the maximal number of pegs strictly between the heights of any two intersection points of \( \widetilde{\gamma} \) with \(\{0\} \times \R\):
    \[
\Ord(K) := \max \left\{ \#\left\{ \text{pegs between } h(p), h(q) \right\} \;\middle|\; p, q \in \widetilde{\gamma} \cap (\{x\} \times \R) \right\}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    The Alexander polynomial determines $\gamma$ by Theorem~\ref{lem:Alex to gamma}. Thus, two generators $x_i$ and $x_{i-1}$ have a height difference of $d_i$. From Equation~\eqref{eq: Ordv from alex} we get that $\Ord(K) = \max\{d_i \mid i = 1, \dots, 2n\}$. Thus, Lemma~\ref{lem:gamma to gC} and Remark~\ref{rem: Curve to ord} yield the result. 
\end{proof}

\begin{rem}
Note that in the proofs for Lemma~\ref{lem:gamma to gC} and Lemma~\ref{lem: pegs to Ord}, it is crucial that $\widetilde{\gamma}$ is a single curve monotone in a neighborhood of the vertical axis, with the first connection $x_0$ to $x_1$ being on the right.
\end{rem}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative}]
    The two key observations for this proof are as follows: 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item If $\widetilde{\gamma}_{p,q}$ is itself an L-space knot, it consists of a single curve that is monotone in a neighborhood of the vertical axis. Consequently, during the process described in Theorem~\ref{prop: cabling process}, two copies of $\widetilde{\gamma}$ do not overlap in height.

        \item Since $p$ and $q$ are coprime, the $p$ copies, each shifted in height by $q$, collectively cover all possible heights without leaving any \emph{gaps}. That is, after the shifts, there will be pegs at every height of the form $n + 1/2$.
    \end{itemize}

    Suppose $g(K) > 1$. Then there exist at least three pegs that lie between the highest and lowest values of $\gr_A(x)$ for all generators. By Proposition~1.1 of~\cite{Krcatovich2018restriction}, the number of pegs between the first and second (resp. the last and second-to-last) intersection is exactly one. Therefore, the maximum number of pegs between two consecutive intersections of $\widetilde{\gamma}_{p,q}$ does not occur at the first or last intersection alone; if it does, it must also occur elsewhere. This maximum configuration is illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:maximum obtained}.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \subfigure[]{%
        \includegraphics[height = 2in]{all_left.png}
    }
    \hspace{2cm}
    \subfigure[]{%
        \includegraphics[height = 2in]{all_right.png}
    }
    \caption{(a) The maximal number of pegs between two intersections is on the left side of $\widetilde{\gamma}$.~(b) The maximal number of pegs is obtained on the right.}
    \label{fig:maximum obtained}
\end{figure}

    Without loss of generality, assume that the maximal number of pegs appears on the right side of the curve $\widetilde{\gamma}$. 

    Consider the copy that is positioned the highest. Because no curve segments are carried from the right, for each peg between the intersections in $\widetilde{\gamma}$, exactly $p$ pegs are introduced in the construction of $\widetilde{\gamma}_{p,q}$. Since the maximum number of pegs occurs on the right $p\cdot \Ord(K)$ pegs are on the vertical line after the shift, along with an additional $p-1$ pegs above the highest peg below the highest intersection. See Figure~\ref{fig:cable construction}.
\begin{figure}[htbp]
    \centering
    \subfigure[]{%
        \includegraphics[height = 3in]{construction_start.png}
    }
    \hspace{0.5cm}
    \unskip\ \vrule\ 
    \hspace{0.5cm}
    \subfigure[]{%
        \includegraphics[height = 3in]{construction_shift.png}
    }
    \hspace{0.5cm}
