    \caption{(a) The cabling construction places lifts of $z \in \partial M$ on staggered heights right of the curve $\widetilde{\gamma}$.~(b) The lifts of $z$ are translated onto the vertical line, dragging the curve along.~(c) The curve $\widetilde{\gamma}_{p,q}$.}
    \label{fig:cable construction}
\end{figure}
Thus, using Lemma~\ref{lem: pegs to Ord} we get 
\begin{align*}
    \Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 \ge ( p \cdot \Ord(K)) + p - 1) + 1 = p \cdot (\Ord(K) + 1).
\end{align*}
If the maximum is attained on the left instead, the lowest copy of $\widetilde{\gamma}$ satisfies the same argument, with pegs being carried from the left accordingly.

    Finally, between the $i$-th and $(i+1)$-th copies of $\widetilde{\gamma}$, the curve $\widetilde{\gamma}_{p,q}$ will have pegs on both sides. The lowest peg in the $i$-th copy will appear on the right (respectively, left) side, implying that the highest peg of the $(i+1)$-th copy will be on the left (respectively, right) side. The $i$-th copy will contain $i$ pegs on the left side, for every multiple of $p$. Similarly, there will be $(p-i)$ pegs on the right side, for every multiple of $p$. Thus, the maximal number of pegs between two successive lifts is $p$.

As established earlier, there will be a section consisting of $p(\Ord(K) + 1) - 1$ pegs on one side of the curve. If $\Ord(K) \geq 1$, it follows that $p(\Ord(K) + 1) - 1 > p$ for $p \geq 2$. The inequality $\Ord(K) \geq 1$ holds because only the unknot satisfies $\Delta_K(t) = 1$, and by Equation~\ref{eq: Ordv from alex}, $\Ord(K)$ is at least one for any other $\Delta_K(t)$ where $K$ is an L-space knot. Thus for $p > 1$,
\begin{align*}
    \Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 = p \cdot (\Ord(K) + 1).
\end{align*}
For $p = 1$, a true cable does not exist, as $K$ is isotopic to $K_{1,q}$, and thus $\Ord(K) + 1= \Ord(K_{1,q}) + 1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{thm: mult for T23}]
    If $g(K) = 1$, the maximal number of pegs between two intersections will not lie in a configuration as in Figure~\ref{fig:maximum obtained}. It spans at least three pegs, but the number of pegs between the highest and lowest intersections is two, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:T23 immersed curve}. However, if $q > 2 \cdot p$, the copies will be staggered in such a way that the highest peg in the $i$-th copy is $p$ units below with the height of the lowest peg of the $(i-1)$-th copy (see Figure~\ref{fig:T23 cable}). This ensures that the argument used in the case $g(K) > 1$ remains applicable. Since $\Ord(K_{2,3}) = 1$, 
    \[
    \Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 = p(\Ord(K) + 1) = 2p. 
    \]

    We still obtain a monotone curve in the case $p < q < 2p$, since the cable $K_{p,q}$ remains an L-space knot. Consider the first copy and focus on the peg at the right of the curve. Due to the cabling construction, there are exactly $p-1$ pegs above this peg in the first copy. To complete the picture, we analyze how many additional pegs lie below the peg of the same copy.

Since the first copy lies furthest to the left, all subsequent pegs are contributions from the right. The first peg, which is left of the curve and below the first copy, corresponds to the highest peg of the second copy, which is shifted downward by $q$ units. Because the construction places one peg at each integer height, there are exactly $q - p - 1$ pegs strictly between the lowest peg of the first copy and the highest peg of the second copy. It follows that
\[
\Ord(K_{p,q}) > q - 1.
\]
As in the previous case, the number of pegs appearing between two consecutive copies is at most $p$, with this maximal length attained only in the topmost and bottommost copies. Since $q > p$, we conclude that equality holds.
    \end{proof}

\section{Discussion}

Theorems~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative} and~\ref{thm: mult for T23} are not sharp. We have identified several instances where multiplicity holds for knots that are not L-space knots, as well as for certain cables of L-space knots that themselves are not L-space knots.

More generally, suppose $d = \Ord(K)$ for a knot $K \subset S^3$, realized by an immersed multicurve with trivial local systems, in a configuration as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:maximum obtained}(a), where the height function restricted to $\gamma$ is injective between two intersection points. Denote the corresponding curve segment by $I$. Then, as Lemma~\ref{lem:gamma to gC} implies, $\operatorname{gC}^-(\widehat{\HF}(S^3 \setminus \operatorname{int}(\nu K)))$ contains a generator $y$ with differential $\partial^-_C y = U^d x$, where $y$ corresponds to the lower and $x$ to the higher intersection. Consequently, $\HFK^-(K)$ has a summand of the form $\F[U]/U^d$.

Now let $K_{p,q}$ be a $(p,q)$-cable of $K$ such that, in the highest copy of $\gamma$ used in the cabling construction of Theorem~\ref{prop: cabling process}, no curve segment is dragged between these intersections, and the image of $I$ under the cabling retains the injectivity of the height function. In this refined setting, the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm: Ord_v is multiplicative} yields the inequality
\begin{equation}
\label{eq: lower bound}
\Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 \geq p\cdot(\Ord(K) + 1).
\end{equation}
What remains open is whether any higher torsion summands can arise under cabling, which would be necessary to attain equality.

\begin{question}
\label{q: setup}
Is this the only configuration that achieves multiplicity of $\Ord(K) + 1$?
\end{question}

\begin{question}
\label{q: maximality}
Does the inequality $\Ord(K_{p,q}) + 1 \leq p \cdot (\Ord(K) + 1)$ hold for all cables?
\end{question}

If both questions have affirmative answers, then Question~\ref{q: mult for cables} also has a positive resolution. In that case, multiplicity for a single cable would imply, by Question~\ref{q: setup}, the existence of a curve segment along which the height function is injective. Because the bounds in Question~\ref{q: mult for cables} are chosen to prevent overlap between distinct copies of $\gamma$ in the cabling construction, this injectivity would persist under cabling, thus we obtain the lower bound as in Inequality~\eqref{eq: lower bound}, and Question~\ref{q: maximality} provides equality.

