\section{ Conclusions}

\label{sec_conclusion}

In this paper, we have analyzed the supersymmetric properties of continuous-time Markov generators
in the spatial dimension $d=1$. In the main text, we have focused on diffusion process involving an arbitrary force $F(x)$ and an arbitrary diffusion coefficient $D(x)$. We have explained how the continuity equation leads to the factorized Fokker-Planck generator ${\cal F}  =  -  \frac{\partial}{\partial x} {\cal J}$ that governs the dynamics of probability $P_t(x)$, while its supersymmetric partner ${\hat {\cal F} }=  - {\cal J}  \frac{\partial}{\partial x}$ governs the dynamics of the current $J_t(x)$.
We have written the two intertwining relations ${\cal J}{\cal F}   =  - {\cal J} \frac{\partial}{\partial x} {\cal J} =  {\hat {\cal F} } {\cal J}$ and ${\cal F} \frac{\partial}{\partial x}  =  -  \frac{\partial}{\partial x} {\cal J} \frac{\partial}{\partial x} = \frac{\partial}{\partial x}{\hat {\cal F} }  $ between ${\cal F} $ and ${\hat {\cal F} } $ in order to relate their right and left eigenvectors. 
We have also described the links with the supersymmetric quantum Hamiltonian $H=Q^{\dagger} Q$ that can be obtained from the Fokker-Planck generator ${\cal F}$ via a similarity transformation, and with the factorization of the adjoint $ {\cal F}^{\dagger} =\frac{d}{d m(x) } \frac{d}{d s(x) } $ of the mathematical literature in terms of the scale function $s(x)$ and speed measure $m(x)$.




Then we have analyzed two possible re-interpretations of the supersymmetric partner ${\hat {\cal F} } = - {\cal J}  \frac{\partial}{\partial x}$ :

 $\bullet$ we have shown how the reinterpretation as the adjoint ${\mathring {\cal F}}^{\dagger} ={\mathring {\cal J} }^{\dagger} \frac{\partial}{\partial x}  $ of  the Fokker-Planck generator ${\mathring {\cal F}}=-  \frac{\partial}{\partial x}  {\mathring {\cal J} }$ associated to the dual force ${\mathring F}(x) =- F(x) -  D'(x)$ is useful to unify and reformulate various known Markov dualities at the level of the operator identity ${\hat {\cal F} } ={\mathring {\cal F}}^{\dagger} $; 

 $\bullet$ we have shown how the reinterpretation as the non-conserved Fokker-Planck generator ${\tilde {\cal F}}_{nc} =  -\frac{\partial}{\partial x} {\tilde {\cal J}} - {\tilde K }(x)$  involving the force $  {\tilde F }(x) =F(x) +D'(x)$ and the killing rate ${\tilde K }(x)=-F'(x)-D''(x)  $ is useful to recover why Pearson diffusions involving a linear force $F(x)$ and a quadratic diffusion coefficient $D(x)$ are exactly solvable for their spectral properties via the shape-invariance involving constant killing rates. 

In the various Appendices, we describe how all these ideas can be also applied to Markov jump processes on the one-dimensional lattice with arbitrary nearest-neighbors transition rates $w(x \pm 1,x)$, via the replacement of derivatives by finite differences.

Our main conclusion is thus that the supersymmetric perspective clarifies the properties of the generators governing
the dynamics of the probabilities $P_t(x)$ and of the currents $J_t(x)$, unifies various notions of Markov dualities,
and directly leads to a simple shape-invariance-exact-solvability for Pearson diffusions.

As recalled in the Introduction, the present paper focusing on $d=1$ can be considered 
as the companion paper of the previous work
\cite{c_susySVD} devoted to the supersymmetric properties of Fokker-Planck generators in dimension $d>1$
(where the current $\vec J_t(\vec x)$ is a d-dimensional vector), and of Markov jump generators on an arbitrary graph
(where the probability $P_t(x)$ lives on the $N$ configurations $x$ while the currents lives on the $M= (N-1)+C$ links between configurations, where $C=M-(N-1)\geq 1$ is the number of independent cycles that may carry steady currents).



